180
00:05:41,932 --> 00:05:44,535
What kind of sick
experiment is going on here?

181
00:05:44,535 --> 00:05:46,394
- [Tom] You know, this
music helps chickens breed.

182
00:05:46,394 --> 00:05:48,250
Kind of like the Luther
Vandross of chickens.

183
00:05:48,250 --> 00:05:52,274
(laughing)
(clucking)

184
00:05:52,274 --> 00:05:53,728
- [Mike] Chickens are dining in front

185
00:05:53,728 --> 00:05:55,213
of a Susan Rothstein mural.

186
00:05:55,213 --> 00:05:56,163
(laughing)

187
00:05:56,163 --> 00:05:58,211
- [Voiceover] After all these
years, whether the chicken

188
00:05:58,211 --> 00:06:00,344
or the egg came first,
is still the subject

189
00:06:00,344 --> 00:06:02,200
of a lot of good-natured debate.

190
00:06:02,200 --> 00:06:03,379
- [Tom] No, it isn't.

191
00:06:03,379 --> 00:06:05,832
- [Voiceover] But one thing
there's no argument about,

192
00:06:05,832 --> 00:06:09,000
chickens and eggs are the
most widely-grown farm product

193
00:06:09,000 --> 00:06:10,739
in the country.

194
00:06:10,739 --> 00:06:13,501
There's not a single
county in the United States

195
00:06:13,501 --> 00:06:15,037
that doesn't raise them.

196
00:06:15,037 --> 00:06:18,302
(growling and munching)

197
00:06:18,302 --> 00:06:19,932
- [Tom] Daddy, must you sit in dinner?

198
00:06:19,932 --> 00:06:21,436
- [Voiceover] In recent
years, this poultry business

199
00:06:21,436 --> 00:06:24,081
has become a big money maker for farmers,

200
00:06:24,081 --> 00:06:25,581
$3 billion a year.

201
00:06:26,470 --> 00:06:29,804
That's second only to dairying
and beef cattle raising.

202
00:06:29,804 --> 00:06:31,636
- [Crow] Woohoo, we're going on a trip!

203
00:06:31,636 --> 00:06:32,660
Hey, where we going?

204
00:06:32,660 --> 00:06:34,010
Woohoo!

205
00:06:34,010 --> 00:06:37,001
- [Tom] Chickens are shipped
to the set of Oklahoma.

206
00:06:37,001 --> 00:06:38,225
(bright orchestra music)

207
00:06:38,225 --> 00:06:40,752
- [Mike] I think the fellow in
white really likes us, Susan.

208
00:06:40,752 --> 00:06:41,810
(squawking)

209
00:06:41,810 --> 00:06:43,953
(bright orchestral music)

210
00:06:43,953 --> 00:06:45,534
- [Voiceover] Just to give
you an idea of the size

211
00:06:45,534 --> 00:06:47,902
of this poultry business, Americans eat

212
00:06:47,902 --> 00:06:50,110
140 million eggs a day.

213
00:06:50,110 --> 00:06:51,838
- [Crow] Oh!

214
00:06:51,838 --> 00:06:55,173
That's one for every man,
woman, and child in the nation.

215
00:06:55,173 --> 00:06:57,093
- These are your chickens on drugs.

216
00:06:57,093 --> 00:06:59,155
- [Voiceover] Chickens, well,
the per capita consumption

217
00:06:59,155 --> 00:07:01,171
is 25 pounds a year.

218
00:07:01,171 --> 00:07:03,726
That means just about
everybody must eat chicken

219
00:07:03,726 --> 00:07:05,788
at least once a week.

220
00:07:05,788 --> 00:07:09,276
Yes, indeed, chickens and
eggs are a big business.

221
00:07:09,276 --> 00:07:11,431
- [Mike] Come on, that tickles.

222
00:07:11,431 --> 00:07:12,572
- [Voiceover] And like big business,

223
00:07:12,572 --> 00:07:15,143
there's a serious effort
to improve the product.

224
00:07:15,143 --> 00:07:16,552
- [Crow] We're removing meal worms.

225
00:07:16,552 --> 00:07:18,408
- [Voiceover] A three-year
program to breed a better chicken

226
00:07:18,408 --> 00:07:19,965
is now being carried on.

227
00:07:19,965 --> 00:07:22,194
- [Tom] Fellow poultrymen,
our cherished freedoms

228
00:07:22,194 --> 00:07:24,584
are threatened from all sides.

229
00:07:24,584 --> 00:07:26,202
- I don't think I've got what it takes

230
00:07:26,202 --> 00:07:27,888
to be the chicken of tomorrow.

231
00:07:27,888 --> 00:07:29,413
- [Mike] I'd like to be
the Chicken of Tomorrow,

232
00:07:29,413 --> 00:07:32,666
but how can I be more the man of today?

233
00:07:32,666 --> 00:07:35,194
- [Voiceover] And on large
and small farms everywhere,

234
00:07:35,194 --> 00:07:37,637
the search for a better chicken goes on.

235
00:07:37,637 --> 00:07:39,126
- [Crow] There it is, wait, no, there!

236
00:07:39,126 --> 00:07:40,157
- [Voiceover] Come
along with us on a visit

237
00:07:40,157 --> 00:07:41,986
to one of the large breeding farms

238
00:07:41,986 --> 00:07:44,280
and see how the old-time barnyard flock

239
00:07:44,280 --> 00:07:45,602
has grown up.

240
00:07:45,602 --> 00:07:46,981
- [Mike] If you're nice, we'll hook you up

241
00:07:46,981 --> 00:07:48,059
to the milking machine.

242
00:07:48,059 --> 00:07:49,339
- [Voiceover] Of course,
they have to be hatched

243
00:07:49,339 --> 00:07:50,640
before they can grow up.

244
00:07:50,640 --> 00:07:53,445
So, let's start at the
beginning, in the incubator.

245
00:07:53,445 --> 00:07:56,128
- [Mike] I'm in the incubator, now.

246
00:07:56,128 --> 00:07:58,315
- [Voiceover] Good chicks
come from good eggs.

247
00:07:58,315 --> 00:07:59,381
- [Crow] Thank you, young man.

248
00:07:59,381 --> 00:08:01,611
- [Voiceover] Fertile eggs
from hens with a pedigree.

249
00:08:01,611 --> 00:08:02,981
- [Crow] Oh, pardon me.
- [Voiceover] The pedigree's

250
00:08:02,981 --> 00:08:06,160
important, because in the
search for a better chicken,

251
00:08:06,160 --> 00:08:07,803
it's breeding that counts.

252
00:08:07,803 --> 00:08:08,987
- [Mike] Hey, can I go to the bathroom?

253
00:08:08,987 --> 00:08:09,937
- [Tom] No, stay in there!

254
00:08:09,937 --> 00:08:11,147
- [Mike] Oh, come on, can I go home?

255
00:08:11,147 --> 00:08:12,797
- [Tom] No, the door's locked.

256
00:08:12,797 --> 00:08:14,699
(bright orchestra music)

257
00:08:14,699 --> 00:08:15,896
- [Crow] I've seen the
episode where the eggs

258
00:08:15,896 --> 00:08:18,403
