putting them in her mouth!

259
00:08:18,403 --> 00:08:19,704
(laughing)

260
00:08:19,704 --> 00:08:21,859
- [Mike] Some eggs are sent to solitary.

261
00:08:21,859 --> 00:08:23,856
- [Voiceover] It's a far
cry from the warm breast

262
00:08:23,856 --> 00:08:26,437
of a broody hen to a modern incubator,

263
00:08:26,437 --> 00:08:30,245
but actually the conditions
are very much the same.

264
00:08:30,245 --> 00:08:33,232
The temperature is held at
99 degrees to approximate

265
00:08:33,232 --> 00:08:34,587
the body heat of the hen.

266
00:08:34,587 --> 00:08:35,995
- [Crow] And this fellow.

267
00:08:35,995 --> 00:08:37,323
- [Voiceover] And a forced draft gives

268
00:08:37,323 --> 00:08:39,285
plenty of ventilation.

269
00:08:39,285 --> 00:08:42,379
This one unit holds 85,000 eggs.

270
00:08:42,379 --> 00:08:43,915
- [Tom] And one bathroom for all them.

271
00:08:43,915 --> 00:08:45,259
(laughing)

272
00:08:45,259 --> 00:08:47,040
- [Voiceover] Did you
ever notice the old hen

273
00:08:47,040 --> 00:08:49,384
turning the eggs over
occasionally as she sets?

274
00:08:49,384 --> 00:08:50,707
- [Crow] Oh yeah, who hasn't?

275
00:08:50,707 --> 00:08:52,723
- [Voiceover] Here, the same
thing is done automatically.

276
00:08:52,723 --> 00:08:54,792
- [Tom] Ride the wild mouse.

277
00:08:54,792 --> 00:08:56,584
- [Voiceover] And now we'l wait 21 days

278
00:08:56,584 --> 00:08:58,024
for things to happen.

279
00:08:58,024 --> 00:08:59,507
- [Crow] Oh, you can't
wait for things to happen,

280
00:08:59,507 --> 00:09:02,270
young man, you've got to make them happen!

281
00:09:02,270 --> 00:09:04,766
- [Voiceover] But thanks to
some very special photography

282
00:09:04,766 --> 00:09:06,614
we can see what's happening.

283
00:09:06,614 --> 00:09:09,348
- [Mike] Oh, this is
gonna be hot, hot, hot.

284
00:09:09,348 --> 00:09:10,841
- [Voiceover] By the end of the third day,

285
00:09:10,841 --> 00:09:12,814
the embryo in the egg has begun to form.

286
00:09:12,814 --> 00:09:14,201
- [Tom] Flock, calling all sons.

287
00:09:14,201 --> 00:09:16,743
- [Crow] Hey, I'm trying to sleep in here.

288
00:09:16,743 --> 00:09:18,834
- [Voiceover] Another
three days, and the heart

289
00:09:18,834 --> 00:09:20,135
has started to beat.

290
00:09:20,135 --> 00:09:21,138
- [Mike] Say, can I have a bigger egg?

291
00:09:21,138 --> 00:09:23,346
- [Tom] That is one bloodshot eye.

292
00:09:23,346 --> 00:09:24,896
- [Mike] Wow.

293
00:09:24,896 --> 00:09:26,799
- [Voiceover] After 10 days, you can see

294
00:09:26,799 --> 00:09:29,050
the chick itself beginning to take shape.

295
00:09:29,050 --> 00:09:31,950
- [Crow] Oh, wait, that's my silly putty.

296
00:09:31,950 --> 00:09:33,991
(mysterious orchestral music)

297
00:09:33,991 --> 00:09:35,644
- [Voiceover] A few
more days and that mass

298
00:09:35,644 --> 00:09:37,735
has some resemblance to a baby chick.

299
00:09:37,735 --> 00:09:40,796
- [Mike] If you kind of squint.

300
00:09:40,796 --> 00:09:41,810
- [Crow] I'll dry eye.

301
00:09:41,810 --> 00:09:45,116
(hiccuping)
(giggling)

302
00:09:45,116 --> 00:09:47,367
- [Voiceover] And finally,
the fully-developed chick

303
00:09:47,367 --> 00:09:49,639
is ready to start
breaking out of its shell.

304
00:09:49,639 --> 00:09:51,634
- [Tom] Sticks of dynamite
are arranged carefully

305
00:09:51,634 --> 00:09:53,384
around the perimeter.

306
00:09:54,258 --> 00:09:55,751
- [Voiceover] At this stage, Mother Nature

307
00:09:55,751 --> 00:09:58,343
gives it a sharp spike
on the end of its beak,

308
00:09:58,343 --> 00:10:01,919
sharp enough to poke a
hole through the shell.

309
00:10:01,919 --> 00:10:04,202
Little by little, the chick works its way

310
00:10:04,202 --> 00:10:05,418
around the shell.

311
00:10:05,418 --> 00:10:06,740
- [Mike] Hold it, I'll be out in a minute.

312
00:10:06,740 --> 00:10:10,772
It'll just take a little
longer than I thought.

313
00:10:10,772 --> 00:10:13,799
- Come on down and meet everybody.

314
00:10:13,799 --> 00:10:16,348
(laughing)

315
00:10:16,348 --> 00:10:17,810
- [Voiceover] Finally the end of the shell

316
00:10:17,810 --> 00:10:20,092
is weakened enough so the
chick can push it out.

317
00:10:20,092 --> 00:10:21,607
- [Tom] Yeah, right, you push it out.

318
00:10:21,607 --> 00:10:22,834
Geez, this is hard.

319
00:10:22,834 --> 00:10:25,532
(grunting)

320
00:10:25,532 --> 00:10:26,599
- [Mike] I hate it when people tape

321
00:10:26,599 --> 00:10:28,946
their own deliveries.

322
00:10:28,946 --> 00:10:31,516
- [Tom] Aren't there supposed
to be pantyhose in there?

323
00:10:31,516 --> 00:10:33,031
- [Mike] Wait, I'm coming, I'm not done.

324
00:10:33,031 --> 00:10:33,864
Ow.

325
00:10:35,710 --> 00:10:37,960
(grunting)

326
00:10:38,948 --> 00:10:40,636
- [Voiceover] And after
a lot of struggling

327
00:10:40,636 --> 00:10:43,581
it tumbles out, exhausted, but free.

328
00:10:43,581 --> 00:10:45,847
- Oh, oh, oh, what did I do last night?

329
00:10:45,847 --> 00:10:47,244
- [Voiceover] It'll rest
now for several hours

330
00:10:47,244 --> 00:10:49,986
and then get up on shaky
legs and begin to live.

331
00:10:49,986 --> 00:10:54,293
- [Mike] For a few
weeks, only to be eaten.

332
00:10:54,293 --> 00:10:55,615
- [Voiceover] Full-time poultrymen

333
00:10:55,615 --> 00:10:57,386
who have been in the
business for many years

334
00:10:57,386 --> 00:10:59,562
say they still get a mighty kick out of

335
00:10:59,562 --> 00:11:01,748
the miracle of the birth of a baby chick.

336
00:11:01,748 --> 00:11:03,970
- [Tom] Hey guys, it's God.

337
00:11:03,970 --> 00:11:05,836
- [Voiceover] The ratio
of pullets and cockerels

338
00:11:05,836 --> 00:11:07,660
among chicks is about 50/50.

339
00:11:07,660 --> 00:11:08,958
- Great, what are those?

340
00:11:08,958 --> 00:11:11,319
- [Voiceover] Of course, you
may order straight run chicks.

341
00:11:11,319 --> 00:11:13,378
Then again, you may order all pullets.

342
00:11:13,378 --> 00:11:14,871
- [Mike] Life is great, it stretches

343
00:11:14,871 --> 00:11:16,610
out in front of me like an eternity.

344
00:11:16,610 --> 00:11:18,167
- [Voiceover] Sexing the
chicks, or separating

345
00:11:18,167 --> 00:11:21,442
the male from the females is
a highly-specialized trade.

346
00:11:21,442 --> 00:11:22,924
- [Tom] Yeah, for pervs.

347
00:11:22,924 --> 00:11:24,228
Whoa, Milton Merl, there.

348
00:11:24,228 --> 00:11:26,535
(laughs)

349
00:11:26,535 --> 00:11:28,786
- [Voiceover] Called for
long experience and training,

350
00:11:28,786 --> 00:11:31,631
but the experts seldom miss.

351
00:11:31,631 --> 00:11:34,554
They can't afford to miss,
because when you order pullets,

352
00:11:34,554 --> 00:11:38,180
you want all pullets, not
a few cockerels mixed in.

353
00:11:38,180 --> 00:11:40,763
- [Mike] Hey, why are we screeching?

354
00:11:40,763 --> 00:11:42,327
(chirping)
- [Crow] Garage sale.

355
00:11:42,327 --> 00:11:43,842
Goodwill.

356
00:11:43,842 --> 00:11:45,800
Save for the kids.

357
00:11:45,800 --> 00:11:48,766
(bright orchestra music)

358
00:11:48,766 --> 00:11:50,455
- [Mike] It's nice, you know it's small,

359
00:11:50,455 --> 00:11:53,036
the walls are neutral.

360
00:11:53,036 --> 00:11:54,914
- [Tom] Oh, hi, Cindy, I'm
so glad I'm in your group.

361
00:11:54,914 --> 00:11:57,458
This is gonna be the fun group.

362
00:11:57,458 --> 00:11:58,692
(chirping)

363
00:11:58,692 --> 00:12:00,676
- [Crow] 40-piece chicken nuggets to go.

364
00:12:00,676 --> 00:12:02,330
- [Voiceover] "But wait a
minute.", you may be saying.

365
00:12:02,330 --> 00:12:03,575
- [Crow] Why am I watching this?

366
00:12:03,575 --> 00:12:04,956
- [Voiceover] Can those
chicks, just out of the shell,

367
00:12:04,956 --> 00:12:07,975
be sent, without food, on trips of a day,

368
00:12:07,975 --> 00:12:09,788
two days, even three?

369
00:12:09,788 --> 00:12:11,132
- [Tom] You bet.

370
00:12:11,132 --> 00:12:12,828
- [Voiceover] Indeed they can.

371
00:12:12,828 --> 00:12:15,058
Nature thought about that, too.

372
00:12:15,058 --> 00:12:17,426
The baby chick has within its little body

373
00:12:17,426 --> 00:12:21,788
enough unabsorbed yoke, to
nourish it for 72 hours,

374
00:12:21,788 --> 00:12:24,740
three days and nights of travel,

375
00:12:24,740 --> 00:12:26,831
with each chick carrying its own lunch.

376
00:12:26,831 --> 00:12:30,640
- [Mike] Then they turn on each other.

377
00:12:30,640 --> 00:12:33,316
- [Voiceover] Nevertheless,
speed is essential

378
00:12:33,316 --> 00:12:35,130
and it's here that the motortruck

379
00:12:35,130 --> 00:12:37,327
plays such a big part in poultry raising.

380
00:12:37,327 --> 00:12:38,818
- I said speed is essential!

381
00:12:38,818 --> 00:12:40,418
- [Voiceover] In fact, the
industry couldn't exist

382
00:12:40,418 --> 00:12:42,956
as it does today without
motor transportation

383
00:12:42,956 --> 00:12:45,580
and the petroleum industry that supplies

384
00:12:45,580 --> 00:12:48,268
the quality fuel and lubricants that make

385
00:12:48,268 --> 00:12:50,871
this high-speed transportation possible.

386
00:12:50,871 --> 00:12:52,823
- [Tom] The unholy
alliance between big oil

387
00:12:52,823 --> 00:12:54,231
and big chickens.

388
00:12:54,231 --> 00:12:56,279
- [Mike] That guy's escaping
disguised as a chicken.

389
00:12:56,279 --> 00:12:57,779
- [Crow] Chickens!

390
00:12:58,743 --> 00:13:00,919
- [Voiceover] But to get
back to our baby chicks.

391
00:13:00,919 --> 00:13:01,783
- [Mike] I'm full.

392
00:13:01,783 --> 00:13:03,591
- [Voiceover] This made a
cute picture, but it's really

393
00:13:03,591 --> 00:13:06,183
not the scientific way to handle chicks.

394
00:13:06,183 --> 00:13:08,728
- [Mike] How many are you sitting on?

395
00:13:08,728 --> 00:13:11,006
- I've been a little nuts this Easter.

396
00:13:11,006 --> 00:13:12,723
- [Voiceover] Their immediate
destination after leaving

397
00:13:12,723 --> 00:13:15,505
the incubator is the brooder house.

398
00:13:15,505 --> 00:13:19,309
- [Mike] Designed by Frank Lloyd Wright.

399
00:13:19,309 --> 00:13:20,909
- [Voiceover] Here a stove is needed

400
00:13:20,909 --> 00:13:23,271
to keep the temperature
up around 95 degrees

401
00:13:23,271 --> 00:13:24,946
for the first week.

402
00:13:24,946 --> 00:13:26,567
Almost any kind of stove will do,

403
00:13:26,567 --> 00:13:28,775
one of those most common
being a kerosene stove.

404
00:13:28,775 --> 00:13:30,170
- Here I come!

405
00:13:30,170 --> 00:13:32,709
- [Tom] Oh, they mingle, get
to know each other a little.

406
00:13:32,709 --> 00:13:34,511
- [Voiceover] The circular
guard is to keep the chicks

407
00:13:34,511 --> 00:13:36,338
near the heat and prevent floor drafts.

408
00:13:36,338 --> 00:13:37,981
- [Tom] There are concerts at the gazebo.

409
00:13:37,981 --> 00:13:39,239
- [Voiceover] For a few days, the litter

410
00:13:39,239 --> 00:13:40,882
should be covered with
paper, so the chicks

411
00:13:40,882 --> 00:13:42,471
don't eat it.

412
00:13:42,471 --> 00:13:45,927
They need plenty of food
and lots and lots of water.

413
00:13:45,927 --> 00:13:47,624
- Individual counseling is provided.

414
00:13:47,624 --> 00:13:49,458
- [Voiceover] From now
on, their main job in life

415
00:13:49,458 --> 00:13:51,314
is to eat and grow.

416
00:13:51,314 --> 00:13:53,814
- [Crow] Eat and grow forever.

417
00:13:54,741 --> 00:13:56,522
- [Voiceover] After the
first few days, the paper

418
00:13:56,522 --> 00:13:57,386
can be removed.

419
00:13:57,386 --> 00:13:58,730
- [Crow] Except for the sports section.

420
00:13:58,730 --> 00:14:00,106
- [Voiceover] Floor should
be covered with litter

421
00:14:00,106 --> 00:14:03,562
to keep the floor warm
and absorb the droppings.

422
00:14:03,562 --> 00:14:08,138
Peat moss, fine-cut straw,
peanut shells, brown corncobs

423
00:14:08,138 --> 00:14:10,527
or anything that will pack rather closely

424
00:14:10,527 --> 00:14:11,530
is good for litter.

425
00:14:11,530 --> 00:14:14,610
- [Crow] Cigarette butts, hair, beer cans.

426
00:14:14,610 --> 00:14:18,027
(upbeat synth pop music)

427
00:14:20,891 --> 00:14:23,522
- [Voiceover] After the chicks
are two or three weeks old,

428
00:14:23,522 --> 00:14:24,941
they can be allowed out of doors,

429
00:14:24,941 --> 00:14:26,007
if the weather's good.

430
00:14:26,007 --> 00:14:29,719
- [Tom] And if they've
completed their lessons.

431
00:14:29,719 --> 00:14:31,021
- [Voiceover] At from eight to 10 weeks,

432
00:14:31,021 --> 00:14:32,898
the pullets being reared
for egg production

433
00:14:32,898 --> 00:14:35,661
are ready to be transferred
from the brooder house

434
00:14:35,661 --> 00:14:36,962
to the range.

435
00:14:36,962 --> 00:14:38,420
- And you know what that means.

436
00:14:38,420 --> 00:14:40,477
(laughing)

437
00:14:40,477 --> 00:14:42,861
- [Voiceover] Range life
is good for chickens.

438
00:14:42,861 --> 00:14:45,194
- Huh, what, what?
- [Mike] What's going on?

439
00:14:45,194 --> 00:14:47,563
- [Tom] What, really, no, you?

440
00:14:47,563 --> 00:14:49,981
- [Voiceover] Range shelter
should be fairly small

441
00:14:49,981 --> 00:14:52,189
holding not more than 100 birds.

442
00:14:52,189 --> 00:14:53,863
- [Tom] Or migrant workers.

443
00:14:53,863 --> 00:14:55,879
- [Voiceover] The shelter
protects them from the sun,

444
00:14:55,879 --> 00:14:57,415
and gives them a safe place to roost

445
00:14:57,415 --> 00:14:58,685
out of the way of rodents.

446
00:14:58,685 --> 00:14:59,518
- Rodents?
- [Tom] Rodents?

447
00:14:59,518 --> 00:15:00,959
(yelling)

448
00:15:00,959 --> 00:15:03,618
- [Mike] Get out, go, go!

449
00:15:03,618 --> 00:15:05,730
- [Voiceover] Proper
diet and plenty of water

450
00:15:05,730 --> 00:15:06,754
are required.

451
00:15:06,754 --> 00:15:07,800
- [Tom] Heavens, I'm so fat, I just look

452
00:15:07,800 --> 00:15:09,346
at chicken feed and I gain weight.

453
00:15:09,346 --> 00:15:10,285
(laughs)

454
00:15:10,285 --> 00:15:11,906
- Does this taste funny to you?

455
00:15:11,906 --> 00:15:13,367
- [Voiceover] A good grass sod is a must

456
00:15:13,367 --> 00:15:15,458
to give the birds an abundant supply

457
00:15:15,458 --> 00:15:17,111
of fresh, tender leaves.

458
00:15:17,111 --> 00:15:19,106
- [Mike] A selection of
ciders and spring water

459
00:15:19,106 --> 00:15:21,197
should be kept on hand.

460
00:15:21,197 --> 00:15:22,250
- [Crow] Normandy.

461
00:15:22,250 --> 00:15:23,837
- [Voiceover] Rain
shelters ought to be spaced

462
00:15:23,837 --> 00:15:26,517
about 75 to 100 feet apart, so then hens

463
00:15:26,517 --> 00:15:30,463
won't go wondering off to
gossip with other flocks.

464
00:15:30,463 --> 00:15:31,765
You know how chickens are.

465
00:15:31,765 --> 00:15:34,442
- [Tom] Yeah, they own
everything, bastards.

466
00:15:34,442 --> 00:15:36,223
- [Voiceover] Oh, it's
a fine life, this life

467
00:15:36,223 --> 00:15:38,869
on the range, but like all good things

468
00:15:38,869 --> 00:15:40,543
it must come to an end.

469
00:15:40,543 --> 00:15:41,523
- [Tom] Time to die.

470
00:15:41,523 --> 00:15:43,298
- [Voiceover] And when they
are about six months old

471
00:15:43,298 --> 00:15:45,277
the pullets are ready to
move to the laying house

472
00:15:45,277 --> 00:15:47,879
to fulfill one of their
main functions in life.

473
00:15:47,879 --> 00:15:51,197
- [Crow] I can't, I've
got a tiny headache.

474
00:15:51,197 --> 00:15:52,541
- [Voiceover] Remember the old hen house?

475
00:15:52,541 --> 00:15:53,607
- [Tom] The one with the rats.

476
00:15:53,607 --> 00:15:55,614
- [Voiceover] It's now
a hotel, a pullet hotel.

477
00:15:55,614 --> 00:15:57,664
- [Tom] Rooms by the hour.

478
00:15:57,664 --> 00:15:59,989
- [Voiceover] Every room
with an outside exposure.

479
00:15:59,989 --> 00:16:02,261
- [Crow] Joey "The Cockscomb" Tortelli.

480
00:16:02,261 --> 00:16:04,358
Ricky "The Bathroom" Shah Vitello.

481
00:16:04,358 --> 00:16:06,865
Jimmy "Crazy Cock" Cuccini.

482
00:16:06,865 --> 00:16:08,817
- [Voiceover] Or if you
prefer the bungalow-type,

483
00:16:08,817 --> 00:16:10,438
we've got that too,

484
00:16:10,438 --> 00:16:12,433
400 feet of it.

485
00:16:12,433 --> 00:16:15,036
All together, room for
thousands of guests.

486
00:16:15,036 --> 00:16:16,433
- [Mike] Or chickens, typically.

487
00:16:16,433 --> 00:16:17,990
- [Voiceover] Seriously,
housing your chickens

488
00:16:17,990 --> 00:16:22,383
is important, whether they're
egg or meat producers,

489
00:16:22,383 --> 00:16:25,381
they aught to have three or
four square feet per bird,

490
00:16:25,381 --> 00:16:26,703
depending on the breed.

491
00:16:26,703 --> 00:16:29,839
- [Crow] Open your hymnals to number 325.

492
00:16:29,839 --> 00:16:31,557
- [Voiceover] They got
to have roosts, too,

493
00:16:31,557 --> 00:16:33,414
but they huddle pretty close together

494
00:16:33,414 --> 00:16:35,983
and two or three birds can
perch on one foot of space.

495
00:16:35,983 --> 00:16:40,150
- [Tom] Everybody, soylent
green is made from chickens!

496
00:16:41,189 --> 00:16:42,795
- [Voiceover] One of the
most important advances

497
00:16:42,795 --> 00:16:46,047
every made in poultry
raising is the trapnest.

498
00:16:46,047 --> 00:16:48,789
The bird can enter the
next easily to lay her egg,

499
00:16:48,789 --> 00:16:51,989
but she can't get out again,
until you let her out.

500
00:16:51,989 --> 00:16:53,749
- [Tom] There's not
point, it's just funny.

501
00:16:53,749 --> 00:16:55,679
- [Voiceover] This simple
device permits you to know

502
00:16:55,679 --> 00:16:57,813
which birds lay which eggs,

503
00:16:57,813 --> 00:17:00,927
and to keep a record of
each hen's egg production.

504
00:17:00,927 --> 00:17:03,370
- [Crow] Which seems
excessively anal-retentive.

505
00:17:03,370 --> 00:17:05,271
- [Voiceover] The whole secret
of profitable chicken raising

506
00:17:05,271 --> 00:17:07,874
is to make them produce
more than they cost to feed.

507
00:17:07,874 --> 00:17:12,450
- [Tom] You think I can wear
these pants out tonight?

508
00:17:12,450 --> 00:17:15,149
- [Voiceover] A hen that
lays 210 eggs a year

509
00:17:15,149 --> 00:17:17,346
and eats 70 pounds of feed, is giving you

510
00:17:17,346 --> 00:17:19,671
three eggs for every pound you feed her.

511
00:17:19,671 --> 00:17:20,930
- [Mike] She will live.

512
00:17:20,930 --> 00:17:21,976
- [Voiceover] Keep that one.

513
00:17:21,976 --> 00:17:24,021
- [Crow] She's worth millions.

514
00:17:24,021 --> 00:17:26,390
- [Voiceover] But if she
eats 70 pounds of feed

515
00:17:26,390 --> 00:17:28,639
and only lays 70 eggs a year,

516
00:17:28,639 --> 00:17:30,133
you better send her to the market

517
00:17:30,133 --> 00:17:31,455
or to your dinner table.

518
00:17:31,455 --> 00:17:34,474
- [Crow] Or put a
warning slip on her desk.

519
00:17:34,474 --> 00:17:36,074
- [Voiceover] And it's
the trapnest that lets you

520
00:17:36,074 --> 00:17:38,517
keep record of how well
each hen is laying.

521
00:17:38,517 --> 00:17:40,093
- [Mike] I'll have an egg
tomorrow, man, I swear.

522
00:17:40,093 --> 00:17:41,363
- [Voiceover] It's men
like this, too, that help

523
00:17:41,363 --> 00:17:43,197
in developing the chicken of tomorrow.

524
00:17:43,197 --> 00:17:46,639
- [Tom] There, go have little fun.

525
00:17:46,639 --> 00:17:47,842
- [Voiceover] You can't
take it for granted

526
00:17:47,842 --> 00:17:49,741
that every hen is earning her keep,

527
00:17:49,741 --> 00:17:51,927
even though laying an egg aught to be easy

528
00:17:51,927 --> 00:17:53,666
for any chicken.

529
00:17:53,666 --> 00:17:56,941
- [Voiceover] That's
what you think, big boy.

530
00:17:56,941 --> 00:17:59,159
- [Crow] That was weird.
- [Mike] Yeah.

531
00:17:59,159 --> 00:18:02,274
- [Voiceover] Well, anyhow,
the trapnest's the answer

532
00:18:02,274 --> 00:18:04,919
to discovering which are the lazy hens,

533
00:18:04,919 --> 00:18:06,829
and this is a good place to point out

534
00:18:06,829 --> 00:18:08,471
a few facts about eggs.

535
00:18:08,471 --> 00:18:10,845
- [Mike] Stop throwing them at my car.

536
00:18:10,845 --> 00:18:12,626
- [Voiceover] The temperature
of an egg when laid

537
00:18:12,626 --> 00:18:14,695
is over 100 degrees.

538
00:18:14,695 --> 00:18:15,922
- [Tom] Oh it's true.

539
00:18:15,922 --> 00:18:17,714
- [Voiceover] Every minute
it's left in a hot nest

540
00:18:17,714 --> 00:18:20,914
in a hot hen house takes
away some of its moisture

541
00:18:20,914 --> 00:18:21,938
and freshness.

542
00:18:21,938 --> 00:18:24,731
- [Crow] So put your
mouth under a chicken.

543
00:18:24,731 --> 00:18:26,715
- [Voiceover] Gather your eggs often,

544
00:18:26,715 --> 00:18:28,134
three or four times a day.

545
00:18:28,134 --> 00:18:31,559
- [Crow] Makes sure to put
them all in one basket.

546
00:18:31,559 --> 00:18:33,872
(bright orchestra music)

547
00:18:33,872 --> 00:18:35,544
- [Voiceover] And they'll
cool out twice as fast

548
00:18:35,544 --> 00:18:37,966
in a wire basket, as in a pail.

549
00:18:37,966 --> 00:18:40,910
- [Tom] These are headed
for the driving range.

550
00:18:40,910 --> 00:18:42,350
- [Voiceover] Clean eggs
bring a better price

551
00:18:42,350 --> 00:18:45,251
than dirty ones and when
you gather them often,

552
00:18:45,251 --> 00:18:46,969
they don't get a chance to get dirty.

553
00:18:46,969 --> 00:18:50,065
- [Tom] Unless filthy
Fred, there, collects them.

554
00:18:50,065 --> 00:18:51,584
- [Voiceover] The secret
of profitable eggs

555
00:18:51,584 --> 00:18:54,464
is to cool them quickly
and keep them cool.

556
00:18:54,464 --> 00:18:56,160
- [Tom] Yeah, I'm cool, baby.

557
00:18:56,160 --> 00:18:57,696
- [Voiceover] And keep them clean.

558
00:18:57,696 --> 00:18:59,960
- [Crow] Lick your eggs or
have a friend lick them.

559
00:18:59,960 --> 00:19:01,891
(laughing)

560
00:19:01,891 --> 00:19:03,160
- [Voiceover] When
you've got as many birds

561
00:19:03,160 --> 00:19:04,835
to look after as this hatchery

562
00:19:04,835 --> 00:19:07,480
your pretty receptive
to labor-saving devices.

563
00:19:07,480 --> 00:19:08,782
- [Tom] Like wagons.

564
00:19:08,782 --> 00:19:11,960
- [Voiceover] And this carrier
system is one of the best.

565
00:19:11,960 --> 00:19:13,688
It runs the length of the building

566
00:19:13,688 --> 00:19:17,027
and is used to carry feed
to the different pens.

567
00:19:17,027 --> 00:19:19,555
It can be used also for
gathering up manure.

568
00:19:19,555 --> 00:19:21,763
Saves a lot of back-breaking work.

569
00:19:21,763 --> 00:19:25,966
- [Tom] Hey, pal, feed me,
then clean up my poops.

570
00:19:25,966 --> 00:19:27,310
- [Crow] There's your appetizers.

571
00:19:27,310 --> 00:19:29,842
I'll be back to get your
drink orders, Ladies.

572
00:19:29,842 --> 00:19:31,174
- [Voiceover] Another labor saver is

573
00:19:31,174 --> 00:19:33,563
the automatic watering trough.

574
00:19:33,563 --> 00:19:35,728
It makes certain there
is always fresh water

575
00:19:35,728 --> 00:19:37,008
for the birds to drink.

576
00:19:37,008 --> 00:19:38,970
- [Mike] We're all wearing Rembrandt hats.

577
00:19:38,970 --> 00:19:40,387
- [Voiceover] Still anther handy gadget

578
00:19:40,387 --> 00:19:41,923
is this grading machine.

579
00:19:41,923 --> 00:19:42,894
- [Crow] Grading!

580
00:19:42,894 --> 00:19:44,131
- [Voiceover] You feed the eggs in

581
00:19:44,131 --> 00:19:45,656
and the machine separates them by weight.

582
00:19:45,656 --> 00:19:48,067
- [Tom] Uh, that one's clean, Roy.

583
00:19:48,067 --> 00:19:49,476
- [Mike] Eggs are complicated.

584
00:19:49,476 --> 00:19:52,260
They should cost like $100 each.

585
00:19:52,260 --> 00:19:53,679
- [Voiceover] The first group will average

586
00:19:53,679 --> 00:19:56,793
24 ounces to the dozen, which
is the most profitable size.

587
00:19:56,793 --> 00:19:59,897
The next, 23 and a half ounces.

588
00:19:59,897 --> 00:20:02,479
The next, 23 and so on.

589
00:20:02,479 --> 00:20:05,711
- [Tom] Some eggs don't even exist.

590
00:20:05,711 --> 00:20:09,305
- [Mike] So you can see why
egg farming is so fascinating.

591
00:20:09,305 --> 00:20:11,439
- [Voiceover] Besides
being a good egg-layer,

592
00:20:11,439 --> 00:20:12,580
the chicken of tomorrow will be

593
00:20:12,580 --> 00:20:14,201
an improved meat producer.

594
00:20:14,201 --> 00:20:15,663
- [Mike] It's Goofus and Gallant.

595
00:20:15,663 --> 00:20:16,847
- [Voiceover] Here's an
example of the progress

596
00:20:16,847 --> 00:20:18,223
that's been made already.

597
00:20:18,223 --> 00:20:19,385
- [Crow] This could be a drumstick.

598
00:20:19,385 --> 00:20:20,825
This is the number to call.

599
00:20:20,825 --> 00:20:22,351
- [Voiceover] Notice how
breeding has increased

600
00:20:22,351 --> 00:20:24,100
the amount of meat on the breast.

601
00:20:24,100 --> 00:20:26,359
- [Tom] Hey, hey, hey, Fat Albert.

602
00:20:26,359 --> 00:20:27,937
- [Voiceover] Look at that drumstick.

603
00:20:27,937 --> 00:20:29,729
- [Crow] Full of salmonella.

604
00:20:29,729 --> 00:20:30,935
- [Voiceover] This bird was fattened

605
00:20:30,935 --> 00:20:32,631
in the same length of time

606
00:20:32,631 --> 00:20:35,863
and on the same amount
of feed as the other one.

607
00:20:35,863 --> 00:20:36,993
Make your own guess as to which

608
00:20:36,993 --> 00:20:38,647
is the more profitable to raise.

609
00:20:38,647 --> 00:20:40,183
- [Tom] Aren't chickens
with good self-esteem

610
00:20:40,183 --> 00:20:41,771
equally important?

611
00:20:41,771 --> 00:20:43,435
- [Voiceover] And when
the chicken of tomorrow

612
00:20:43,435 --> 00:20:45,781
gets to the dinner table, its advantages

613
00:20:45,781 --> 00:20:47,296
are still more apparent.

614
00:20:47,296 --> 00:20:51,144
- [Mike] These chickens
were smoking in bed.

615
00:20:51,144 --> 00:20:55,311
- [Crow] May I have a piece
of my own white meat, please?

616
00:20:56,589 --> 00:20:59,074
- [Tom] Oh, thanks for
the generous helping.

617
00:20:59,074 --> 00:21:00,653
Geez.

618
00:21:00,653 --> 00:21:02,178
- [Mike] Uh, Dad, if you
could carve a little faster

619
00:21:02,178 --> 00:21:03,786
I'm kind of hungry.

620
00:21:03,786 --> 00:21:06,242
(grand orchestral music)

621
00:21:06,242 --> 00:21:07,954
- [Crow] Yes, chicken sliced to the width

622
00:21:07,954 --> 00:21:09,682
of one electron.

623
00:21:09,682 --> 00:21:13,512
- [Tom] We must be in
models portions, here.

624
00:21:13,512 --> 00:21:17,262
- [Mike] Miss Moss, and
for you, Miss Taylor.

625
00:21:18,274 --> 00:21:20,442
- [Crow] Yes, it's chicken,

626
00:21:20,442 --> 00:21:22,138
glorious American chicken sliced

627
00:21:22,138 --> 00:21:23,600
the American way.

628
00:21:23,600 --> 00:21:26,350
- [Mike] Carry it in your wallet.

629
00:21:27,206 --> 00:21:29,625
- [Voiceover] But it's your
pocketbook that profits most

630
00:21:29,625 --> 00:21:32,322
when you send this bird to market.

631
00:21:32,322 --> 00:21:33,901
- [Mike] We bring you now to market.

632
00:21:33,901 --> 00:21:35,789
- [Voiceover] A unique
method of marketing chickens

633
00:21:35,789 --> 00:21:38,083
and eggs that's grown in popularity

634
00:21:38,083 --> 00:21:40,056
in recent years is the aucition.

635
00:21:40,056 --> 00:21:42,477
- [Tom] Such as here, at Sutheby's.

636
00:21:42,477 --> 00:21:43,917
- [Voiceover] The first
one is said to have been

637
00:21:43,917 --> 00:21:46,393
the Hunterdon County at
Flemington, New Jersey.

638
00:21:46,393 --> 00:21:47,746
- [Mike] Oh, that's an urban myth.

639
00:21:47,746 --> 00:21:48,792
- [Voiceover] But the idea has spread

640
00:21:48,792 --> 00:21:50,093
to all parts of the country.

641
00:21:50,093 --> 00:21:52,781
- [Crow] The chicken shortage of 1937.

642
00:21:52,781 --> 00:21:54,381
- [Voiceover] The farmers
bring their crates of eggs

643
00:21:54,381 --> 00:21:56,130
to market the day before the auction.

644
00:21:56,130 --> 00:21:57,592
- [Tom] Alternatively,
they may bring their eggs

645
00:21:57,592 --> 00:22:00,009
early the day of the auction.

646
00:22:01,048 --> 00:22:03,160
- [Voiceover] On auction
day, the buyers gather

647
00:22:03,160 --> 00:22:05,325
to purchase eggs and poultry at prices

648
00:22:05,325 --> 00:22:06,999
opening arrived at.

649
00:22:06,999 --> 00:22:07,962
- [Auctioneer] Bid two, two.

650
00:22:07,962 --> 00:22:09,538
Two over there, two's the bid.

651
00:22:09,538 --> 00:22:10,371
It's amazing.

652
00:22:10,371 --> 00:22:11,204
- [Mike] You're adorable.

653
00:22:11,204 --> 00:22:12,255
- 17 a quarter and a half.

654
00:22:12,255 --> 00:22:13,204
It's 17 and a half.

655
00:22:13,204 --> 00:22:15,780
(yelling)

656
00:22:15,780 --> 00:22:16,613
- [Mike] Ross Perot or something.

657
00:22:16,613 --> 00:22:17,527
- Three quarters the bid.

658
00:22:17,527 --> 00:22:20,045
Two, twos the bid, now two and a quarter.

659
00:22:20,045 --> 00:22:21,886
- [Tom] He's drunk.

660
00:22:21,886 --> 00:22:23,828
- Here, 17's the bid!

661
00:22:23,828 --> 00:22:26,853
(speaking gibberish)

662
00:22:26,853 --> 00:22:27,686
(clapping)

663
00:22:27,686 --> 00:22:29,819
- One and a half, one and a half, $1.75.

664
00:22:29,819 --> 00:22:31,652
- [Tom] Get your hand up.

665
00:22:31,652 --> 00:22:32,485
Buy a chicken.

666
00:22:32,485 --> 00:22:34,290
- [Mike] I don't want one.

667
00:22:34,290 --> 00:22:36,887
- [Voiceover] And after the
eggs and poultry are sold

668
00:22:36,887 --> 00:22:40,653
once again the motortruck
plays its big role.

669
00:22:40,653 --> 00:22:42,253
- [Crow] Alright, come on, sing, sing.

670
00:22:42,253 --> 00:22:44,543
Row, row, row, you're not singing!

671
00:22:44,543 --> 00:22:46,591
- [Voiceover] Hundreds of
live chickens can be speeded

672
00:22:46,591 --> 00:22:48,031
on their way to the dressing plant.

673
00:22:48,031 --> 00:22:49,791
- [Crow] The dressing
plant, sounds like fun.

674
00:22:49,791 --> 00:22:51,341
- [Tom] I want a new hat.

675
00:22:51,341 --> 00:22:53,418
(brassy orchestral music)

676
00:22:53,418 --> 00:22:55,559
- [Voiceover] One truck can
handle thousands of eggs

677
00:22:55,559 --> 00:22:57,555
and take them anywhere there's a market.

678
00:22:57,555 --> 00:23:00,816
- [Mike] Even to the Texaco station.

679
00:23:00,816 --> 00:23:03,995
- [Voiceover] Speed and
flexibility, that's what

680
00:23:03,995 --> 00:23:07,067
the industry must have
in it's transportation.

681
00:23:07,067 --> 00:23:08,067
- [Tom] You?

682
00:23:08,901 --> 00:23:11,867
- [Voiceover] And that's
exactly what the motortruck,

683
00:23:11,867 --> 00:23:13,168
fueled and lubricated with

684
00:23:13,168 --> 00:23:16,859
quality petroleum products, offers.

685
00:23:16,859 --> 00:23:19,067
(snoring)

686
00:23:19,067 --> 00:23:22,216
- [Crow] Buddy, buddy, wake up.

687
00:23:22,216 --> 00:23:23,899
Hey, buddy.

688
00:23:23,899 --> 00:23:25,501
- [Voiceover] Ask any
poultryman and he'll tell you

689
00:23:25,501 --> 00:23:28,659
that the industry could not
flourish as it's doing today

690
00:23:28,659 --> 00:23:31,187
without the efficient fleets of trucks

691
00:23:31,187 --> 00:23:33,396
that whisk baby chicks to waiting farmers.

692
00:23:33,396 --> 00:23:34,324
- [Tom] To be killed.

693
00:23:34,324 --> 00:23:35,632
- [Voiceover] Rush
chickens to the grocer's

694
00:23:35,632 --> 00:23:36,465
and butcher's shelves.

695
00:23:36,465 --> 00:23:37,298
- [Tom] To be killed.

696
00:23:37,298 --> 00:23:38,622
- [Voiceover] And speed fresh eggs

697
00:23:38,622 --> 00:23:40,180
to your breakfast table.

698
00:23:40,180 --> 00:23:41,737
- [Mike] Did America
really need to be sold

699
00:23:41,737 --> 00:23:43,988
on the automotive industry at this point?

700
00:23:43,988 --> 00:23:44,958
- [Crow] There's no driver.

701
00:23:44,958 --> 00:23:46,558
The chickens are taking over!

702
00:23:46,558 --> 00:23:47,391
Woohoo.

703
00:23:48,745 --> 00:23:52,912
(squawking)
(old orchestral music)

704
00:23:58,866 --> 00:24:02,780
(creaking)
(bolts clanking)

705
00:24:02,780 --> 00:24:04,271
(beeping)
(engine whirring)

706
00:24:04,271 --> 00:24:06,628
(door sliding)
(chains clinking)

707
00:24:06,628 --> 00:24:09,710
(door slamming)
(metal scraping)

708
00:24:09,710 --> 00:24:11,503
(door closes)

709
00:24:11,503 --> 00:24:13,318
- Should I be sitting astride you

710
00:24:13,318 --> 00:24:16,614
with my warm and nurturing
hindquarters or anything?

711
00:24:16,614 --> 00:24:18,630
- [Tom] No, no, I'm just fine.

712
00:24:18,630 --> 00:24:19,590
- How-de-do, Crow.

713
00:24:19,590 --> 00:24:20,934
Hey have you seen Tom around anywhere?

714
00:24:20,934 --> 00:24:22,140
- [Tom] Hello, Mike,

715
00:24:22,140 --> 00:24:23,420
I'm in here!

716
00:24:23,420 --> 00:24:24,294
(laughing)

717
00:24:24,294 --> 00:24:25,649
- Hey, Tom, how are you
doing, you okay in there?

718
00:24:25,649 --> 00:24:27,174
- [Tom] Oh, more than
okay, Mike, I'm floating

719
00:24:27,174 --> 00:24:29,097
in a sea of warm nutrients and I'm all set

720
00:24:29,097 --> 00:24:31,284
to begin my 21 day gestation period.

721
00:24:31,284 --> 00:24:34,025
I want to experience the
miracle of chicken birth, Mike.

722
00:24:34,025 --> 00:24:35,465
- Yeah, plus, there's a huge market

723
00:24:35,465 --> 00:24:37,044
for them egg-hatching movies.

724
00:24:37,044 --> 00:24:39,454
So we got Cambot filming the whole thing.

725
00:24:39,454 --> 00:24:40,436
- [Tom] Yep, yep.

726
00:24:40,436 --> 00:24:41,662
- Yeah, that's, that's great.

727
00:24:41,662 --> 00:24:43,102
You know, it really is
beautiful, too, Tom,

728
00:24:43,102 --> 00:24:45,652
but I wish you'd told me
about it before you did this.

729
00:24:45,652 --> 00:24:46,869
- [Tom] Really, why?

730
00:24:46,869 --> 00:24:48,862
- Well, you're the only one that, hello?

731
00:24:48,862 --> 00:24:50,420
- [Tom] Yeah, go on with your story.

732
00:24:50,420 --> 00:24:52,062
- Well, I was gonna say,
three weeks without you,

733
00:24:52,062 --> 00:24:53,950
you're the only one I
depend on for any kind

734
00:24:53,950 --> 00:24:55,593
of urbane conversation.

735
00:24:55,593 --> 00:24:56,894
- [Tom] Well, I understand, Mike,

736
00:24:56,894 --> 00:24:57,897
but it's too late now.

737
00:24:57,897 --> 00:25:00,382
Just put me in a warm place,
soak me in some enzymes.

738
00:25:00,382 --> 00:25:01,796
- No, you know what, I
gotta get you out of there.

739
00:25:01,796 --> 00:25:02,746
I can't live without you.

740
00:25:02,746 --> 00:25:04,190
- [Tom] No, no, Mike, Mike,
I'm not ready to hatch yet.

741
00:25:04,190 --> 00:25:05,348
I'm not fully formed, Mike!
- [Crow] Mike, be careful!

742
00:25:05,348 --> 00:25:06,425
Be careful!
- [Tom] Ahhh!

743
00:25:06,425 --> 00:25:07,850
(splashing)

744
00:25:07,850 --> 00:25:08,908
- Oh.
- [Crow] Oh, uhh.

745
00:25:08,908 --> 00:25:09,882
- What a mess.

746
00:25:09,882 --> 00:25:11,001
(coughing and grunting)
We'll be right back.

747
00:25:11,001 --> 00:25:12,517
- I'll go get a whole
bunch of paper towels.

748
00:25:12,517 --> 00:25:13,508
- Alright, alright.

749
00:25:13,508 --> 00:25:15,361
- [Tom] Oh boy.

750
00:25:15,361 --> 00:25:18,194
(synth pop music)

751
00:25:21,431 --> 00:25:23,863
(old orchestral music)

752
00:25:23,863 --> 00:25:25,516
