00:34:20,747 --> 00:34:24,592
- [Tom] Gotta snag me
some Jimmy hats, pronto.

1006
00:34:24,592 --> 00:34:27,494
- [Crow] Just drop off my prescription.

1007
00:34:27,494 --> 00:34:28,710
- [Mike] He's one of those weird guys

1008
00:34:28,710 --> 00:34:30,054
who hangs around on a college scene

1009
00:34:30,054 --> 00:34:31,334
long after he graduates.

1010
00:34:31,334 --> 00:34:32,912
- [Crow] Yeah, sad.

1011
00:34:32,912 --> 00:34:35,329
- [Tom] Welcome back to 1910.

1012
00:34:36,800 --> 00:34:38,296
- [Voiceover] A woman was
murdered in the same way

1013
00:34:38,296 --> 00:34:39,832
as the other four victims.

1014
00:34:39,832 --> 00:34:41,731
Authorities have issued
a warning to all citizens

1015
00:34:41,731 --> 00:34:43,587
to be on the lookout for this fiend.

1016
00:34:43,587 --> 00:34:44,590
- [Crow] Do, be.

1017
00:34:44,590 --> 00:34:45,518
- [Voiceover] As far as the police know,

1018
00:34:45,518 --> 00:34:46,862
he only appears at night.

1019
00:34:46,862 --> 00:34:48,974
He's a large, heavyset man usually wearing

1020
00:34:48,974 --> 00:34:50,723
a black coat and hat.

1021
00:34:50,723 --> 00:34:53,176
Descriptions of the
creeper have been submitted

1022
00:34:53,176 --> 00:34:55,224
to headquarters, but these
vary to such an extent

1023
00:34:55,224 --> 00:34:56,664
that it's hard to give an accurate picture

1024
00:34:56,664 --> 00:34:57,635
of the man's face.

1025
00:34:57,635 --> 00:34:59,150
- [Mike] Oh, come on, what'd the twins do?

1026
00:34:59,150 --> 00:35:00,611
- [Voiceover] Last seen, he
was on the tenement rooftop

1027
00:35:00,611 --> 00:35:01,656
near the waterfront.

1028
00:35:01,656 --> 00:35:02,584
- [Crow] He was tanning.

1029
00:35:02,584 --> 00:35:03,417
- [Voiceover] And now, turning to

1030
00:35:03,417 --> 00:35:04,262
the other news of the day.

1031
00:35:04,262 --> 00:35:05,360
- [Mr. Haskins] Jimmy!

1032
00:35:05,360 --> 00:35:06,587
- Good morning, Mr. Haskins.

1033
00:35:06,587 --> 00:35:08,027
- Leave that radio alone.

1034
00:35:08,027 --> 00:35:09,328
Sweep up the place.

1035
00:35:09,328 --> 00:35:12,443
You're getting paid to work,
not entertain yourself.

1036
00:35:12,443 --> 00:35:14,779
- Okay, but that creeper guy murdered

1037
00:35:14,779 --> 00:35:16,176
somebody again last night.

1038
00:35:16,176 --> 00:35:19,152
- Oh, murderers, detectives, gangsters.

1039
00:35:19,152 --> 00:35:20,966
That's all you think about.

1040
00:35:20,966 --> 00:35:21,915
Get the broom.

1041
00:35:21,915 --> 00:35:24,498
- [Tom] I think about sex, too.

1042
00:35:26,220 --> 00:35:28,224
- [Mike] Eat me, a hateful old fossil.

1043
00:35:28,224 --> 00:35:29,174
Signed, Jimmy.

1044
00:35:29,174 --> 00:35:30,007
Hey.

1045
00:35:30,007 --> 00:35:32,630
- [Crow] Hey, the brute
has fourth grade cursive.

1046
00:35:32,630 --> 00:35:35,130
- [Tom] Signed, not the brute.

1047
00:35:36,416 --> 00:35:37,440
- [Jimmy] Yeah?

1048
00:35:37,440 --> 00:35:38,411
- [Crow] I didn't say anything.

1049
00:35:38,411 --> 00:35:39,318
- Where'd this come from?

1050
00:35:39,318 --> 00:35:40,918
- Somebody stuck it under the door.

1051
00:35:40,918 --> 00:35:41,751
- Oh.

1052
00:35:41,751 --> 00:35:43,030
- [Crow] I hate customers!

1053
00:35:43,030 --> 00:35:45,099
- Don't you think it's kind of funny?

1054
00:35:45,099 --> 00:35:45,932
(giggling)

1055
00:35:45,932 --> 00:35:46,891
They leave a note under the door?

1056
00:35:46,891 --> 00:35:47,724
- No.

1057
00:35:47,724 --> 00:35:48,557
- [Tom] Go to hell.

1058
00:35:48,557 --> 00:35:49,750
- And don't go trying to
make a mystery out of it.

1059
00:35:49,750 --> 00:35:50,838
- [Crow] Piss off!

1060
00:35:50,838 --> 00:35:53,558
- Somebody probably too
busy to pick up the stuff.

1061
00:35:53,558 --> 00:35:54,688
- Could be the creeper.

1062
00:35:54,688 --> 00:35:56,896
- Creeper, creeper, creeper.

1063
00:35:56,896 --> 00:35:58,475
You give me the creeps.

1064
00:35:58,475 --> 00:35:59,308
- [Crow] Zinga.

1065
00:35:59,308 --> 00:36:00,406
- Well, it could be.

1066
00:36:00,406 --> 00:36:01,611
That'd be a swell reason why he wouldn't

1067
00:36:01,611 --> 00:36:03,190
want to see anybody or come out,

1068
00:36:03,190 --> 00:36:04,950
except at night.

1069
00:36:04,950 --> 00:36:07,016
- You just gotta deliver these groceries,

1070
00:36:07,016 --> 00:36:09,310
and don't forget the money.

1071
00:36:09,310 --> 00:36:10,494
A dollar and a quarter.

1072
00:36:10,494 --> 00:36:12,307
- [Mike] Why do you hire
me if you hate me so?

1073
00:36:12,307 --> 00:36:14,867
- Okay, but I still think it might--

1074
00:36:14,867 --> 00:36:17,907
- I know, so he's the creeper.

1075
00:36:17,907 --> 00:36:19,486
You just creep along with that,

1076
00:36:19,486 --> 00:36:21,182
I mean, hurry up with that stuff,

1077
00:36:21,182 --> 00:36:22,942
and then get back here and
do the rest of your work!

1078
00:36:22,942 --> 00:36:24,499
- [Crow] Dear God, I hate you!

1079
00:36:24,499 --> 00:36:26,515
I hope you die!

1080
00:36:26,515 --> 00:36:27,838
- [Tom] Crusty little bastard, probably

1081
00:36:27,838 --> 00:36:30,238
needs somebody to whip.

1082
00:36:30,238 --> 00:36:31,088
(laughing)

1083
00:36:31,088 --> 00:36:32,784
(ominous orchestral music)

1084
00:36:32,784 --> 00:36:36,246
- [Mike] Hey a pony keg, alright.

1085
00:36:36,246 --> 00:36:37,464
- [Tom] Oh, it's just magnificent when

1086
00:36:37,464 --> 00:36:40,227
the tall ship come into the harbor.

1087
00:36:40,227 --> 00:36:43,110
- [Crow] Meanwhile, on Il Trovatore.

1088
00:36:43,110 --> 00:36:45,531
(ominous orchestral music)

1089
00:36:45,531 --> 00:36:47,795
- [Mike] Okay, let me
see 23 Pestilence Way.

1090
00:36:47,795 --> 00:36:50,003
(knocking)

1091
00:36:50,003 --> 00:36:51,507
- [Brute Man] Leave it outside.

1092
00:36:51,507 --> 00:36:53,811
- [Tom] Hello, Dolly.

1093
00:36:53,811 --> 00:36:55,390
(paper rustling)

1094
00:36:55,390 --> 00:36:56,243
- [Brute Man] How much?

1095
00:36:56,243 --> 00:36:58,326
- A dollar and a quarter.

1096
00:36:59,294 --> 00:37:01,082
(door opens)
- [Crow] Here's a toenail.

1097
00:37:01,082 --> 00:37:02,456
(door closes)

1098
00:37:02,456 --> 00:37:03,758
- [Brute Man] Go away.

1099
00:37:03,758 --> 00:37:06,126
- [Mike] Have a nice day.

1100
00:37:06,126 --> 00:37:08,750
- [Tom] Shucks, I guess
no one likes me today.

1101
00:37:08,750 --> 00:37:09,583
- [Crow] Is it me?

1102
00:37:09,583 --> 00:37:12,166
Am I sending off hostile vibes?

1103
00:37:13,833 --> 00:37:15,216
(door opens)

1104
00:37:15,216 --> 00:37:17,078
(door creaking)

1105
00:37:17,078 --> 00:37:18,422
- [Mike] I really
shouldn't do this when I'm

1106
00:37:18,422 --> 00:37:19,672
on the crapper.

1107
00:37:20,622 --> 00:37:23,744
(door creaking)

1108
00:37:23,744 --> 00:37:25,198
- [Tom] Man, this menu's hard to read.

1109
00:37:25,198 --> 00:37:27,512
Let's see, lobster, 24 bucks?

1110
00:37:27,512 --> 00:37:28,792
Wow.

1111
00:37:28,792 --> 00:37:31,139
(paper rustling)

1112
00:37:31,139 --> 00:37:34,972
- [Crow] Aw, great, eight
o'clock coffee, god.

1113
00:37:36,248 --> 00:37:39,498
- [Tom] They forgot my fruit brute cereal.

1114
00:37:39,498 --> 00:37:42,819
(ominous orchestral music)

1115
00:37:42,819 --> 00:37:43,652
(gasps)

1116
00:37:43,652 --> 00:37:45,402
the Shroud of Turine.

1117
00:37:46,267 --> 00:37:48,496
- [Crow] Say, you might
want to check the freshness

1118
00:37:48,496 --> 00:37:49,819
on that mayonnaise.

1119
00:37:49,819 --> 00:37:52,986
I got it out of the back and, hey.

1120
00:37:52,986 --> 00:37:55,619
- [Tom] Should I have tipped him?

1121
00:37:55,619 --> 00:37:57,910
(ominous orchestral music)

1122
00:37:57,910 --> 00:38:02,310
- [Crow] He's Big Head
Todd and the Monster.

1123
00:38:02,310 --> 00:38:05,601
- [Tom] Hey, take two, look at that.

1124
00:38:05,601 --> 00:38:08,525
(ominous orchestral music)

1125
00:38:08,525 --> 00:38:09,991
- [Mike] Okay, okay, okay, don't blow it.

1126
00:38:09,991 --> 00:38:13,031
I've got a real possibility
of a free back break here.

1127
00:38:13,031 --> 00:38:14,673
I'm gonna breathe.

1128
00:38:14,673 --> 00:38:17,159
- [Crow] A scene from Brother's Keeper.

1129
00:38:17,159 --> 00:38:18,737
- [Tom] Alright, relax,
it's about something

1130
00:38:18,737 --> 00:38:21,686
we haven't done a whole
lot of times before.

1131
00:38:21,686 --> 00:38:23,826
(ominous orchestral music)

1132
00:38:23,826 --> 00:38:24,860
- [Mike] Okay you're there.

1133
00:38:24,860 --> 00:38:26,236
Stay out of your own head, now,

1134
00:38:26,236 --> 00:38:29,627
hands up to ten and two,
into the creeping position.

1135
00:38:29,627 --> 00:38:31,695
(scary orchestral music)

1136
00:38:31,695 --> 00:38:32,748
- [Crow] A promising delivering career

1137
00:38:32,748 --> 00:38:34,145
comes to an end.

1138
00:38:34,145 --> 00:38:36,001
- [Tom] There's very little
chance he flanked me.

1139
00:38:36,001 --> 00:38:39,244
I can't imagine him doing that.

1140
00:38:39,244 --> 00:38:40,753
(muffled yelling)

1141
00:38:40,753 --> 00:38:43,537
- [Mike] Hey, your hand
smells like pennies.

1142
00:38:43,537 --> 00:38:46,065
- [Crow] And some prune
juice, Metamucil, Correctol,

1143
00:38:46,065 --> 00:38:48,924
Fibercon, Go Easy, and then, thank you.

1144
00:38:48,924 --> 00:38:51,151
- Thank you, Miss Hart.

1145
00:38:51,151 --> 00:38:52,687
(paper rustling)

1146
00:38:52,687 --> 00:38:54,071
- [Mike] Weathered bitch.

1147
00:38:54,071 --> 00:38:54,904
- Oh, boy.

1148
00:38:57,527 --> 00:39:00,829
- [Mike] You know, by itself,
that doesn't establish much.

1149
00:39:00,829 --> 00:39:02,706
- You'll send Jimmy over
with the rest of that order

1150
00:39:02,706 --> 00:39:03,983
won't you, Mr. Haskins?

1151
00:39:03,983 --> 00:39:05,167
- Yes, I will, if he ever gets back from

1152
00:39:05,167 --> 00:39:06,745
that errand I sent him on.

1153
00:39:06,745 --> 00:39:08,281
He's been gone nearly two hours.

1154
00:39:08,281 --> 00:39:09,604
- Well, that's the boy for you.

1155
00:39:09,604 --> 00:39:11,343
These days, they can't
seem to keep their minds

1156
00:39:11,343 --> 00:39:12,324
on their work.

1157
00:39:12,324 --> 00:39:13,177
Good day, Mr. Haskins.

1158
00:39:13,177 --> 00:39:14,010
- Good day, Miss Hart.

1159
00:39:14,010 --> 00:39:18,055
- [Tom] Let's go out,
be crotchety sometime.

1160
00:39:18,055 --> 00:39:21,727
- [Mike] Oh man, she's
a fine piece of woman.

1161
00:39:21,727 --> 00:39:22,825
- [Crow] God is dead?

1162
00:39:22,825 --> 00:39:23,841
Good! Ha!

1163
00:39:23,841 --> 00:39:26,198
(ominous orchestral music)

1164
00:39:26,198 --> 00:39:27,391
- [Tom] Ha, look at that.

1165
00:39:27,391 --> 00:39:28,756
Philanthropist laid.

1166
00:39:28,756 --> 00:39:30,292
It's always a philanthropist.

1167
00:39:30,292 --> 00:39:33,292
Rock stars and philanthropists, wow.

1168
00:39:34,239 --> 00:39:37,205
- [Mike] Erma Bombeck has a good point.

1169
00:39:37,205 --> 00:39:39,252
(ominous orchestral music)

1170
00:39:39,252 --> 00:39:42,012
- [Crow] How about Captain
Flash or Captain Rocket?

1171
00:39:42,012 --> 00:39:43,537
Captain M.J. Donelly just doesn't do it

1172
00:39:43,537 --> 00:39:44,836
for a superhero.

1173
00:39:44,836 --> 00:39:46,276
(door closes)

1174
00:39:46,276 --> 00:39:47,817
How are things in homicide?

1175
00:39:47,817 --> 00:39:49,321
- Good morning, Captain.

1176
00:39:49,321 --> 00:39:50,740
- I'll bet you think it's good.

1177
00:39:50,740 --> 00:39:51,583
- [Tom] You.

1178
00:39:51,583 --> 00:39:52,905
- But does that beaming purse of yours

1179
00:39:52,905 --> 00:39:54,345
mean that you have some good news?

1180
00:39:54,345 --> 00:39:56,148
- Huh-uh, bad news.

1181
00:39:56,148 --> 00:39:57,268
Its on the way.

1182
00:39:57,268 --> 00:39:58,943
I just saw Commissioner Salisbury

1183
00:39:58,943 --> 00:40:00,508
parking his car outside.

1184
00:40:00,508 --> 00:40:02,440
The mayor's secretary is with him.

1185
00:40:02,440 --> 00:40:03,891
Just thought I'd let you know, give you

1186
00:40:03,891 --> 00:40:05,631
at least two minutes to
think up a good story.

1187
00:40:05,631 --> 00:40:06,537
- [Mike] Nah, nah, nah.

1188
00:40:06,537 --> 00:40:09,673
- Hold it, Lieutenant, come back here.

1189
00:40:09,673 --> 00:40:10,601
- Who, me?

1190
00:40:10,601 --> 00:40:11,551
- Yeah, you.

1191
00:40:11,551 --> 00:40:13,343
- [Crow] Who put the
cookie in the cookie jar?

1192
00:40:13,343 --> 00:40:16,244
- After all, I assigned
you to the creeper murders,

1193
00:40:16,244 --> 00:40:19,657
and I need a partner in this
little game of buck passing.

1194
00:40:19,657 --> 00:40:22,387
By the way, just what have you done toward

1195
00:40:22,387 --> 00:40:23,361
apprehending the murderer?

1196
00:40:23,361 --> 00:40:24,247
- Are you kidding?

1197
00:40:24,247 --> 00:40:25,080
You know what we've done.

1198
00:40:25,080 --> 00:40:26,460
We're operating the biggest dragnet

1199
00:40:26,460 --> 00:40:27,796
in the history of crime in this city.

1200
00:40:27,796 --> 00:40:28,841
- Well, it isn't big enough, you haven't

1201
00:40:28,841 --> 00:40:29,674
caught the creeper yet.

1202
00:40:29,674 --> 00:40:30,507
(door opens)

1203
00:40:30,507 --> 00:40:32,188
I want every available
man in the homicide burrow

1204
00:40:32,188 --> 00:40:33,553
put to work on this case immediately.

1205
00:40:33,553 --> 00:40:34,386
- Now wait, I--

1206
00:40:34,386 --> 00:40:35,505
- I don't want excuses.

1207
00:40:35,505 --> 00:40:37,553
I want the creeper in
jail within 24 hours,

1208
00:40:37,553 --> 00:40:39,644
or they'll be some
changes made around here.

1209
00:40:39,644 --> 00:40:40,828
- [Tom] Yeah, midgets.

1210
00:40:40,828 --> 00:40:42,012
- Oh, good morning, Commissioner.

1211
00:40:42,012 --> 00:40:43,196
Good morning, Mr. Parkington.

1212
00:40:43,196 --> 00:40:44,029
- Good morning.

1213
00:40:44,029 --> 00:40:45,073
- Captain Donelly.

1214
00:40:45,073 --> 00:40:47,260
- Your looking fine, Commissioner.

1215
00:40:47,260 --> 00:40:48,444
And how's the Missus?

1216
00:40:48,444 --> 00:40:50,577
I trust she's feeling as good you look.

1217
00:40:50,577 --> 00:40:52,113
- She's not, she's scared to death

1218
00:40:52,113 --> 00:40:53,553
like every other woman in town,

1219
00:40:53,553 --> 00:40:56,689
since wholesale murder has
the peace departments stymied.

1220
00:40:56,689 --> 00:40:59,004
- I presume you refer
to the creeper murders.

1221
00:40:59,004 --> 00:41:00,561
- You presume right.

1222
00:41:00,561 --> 00:41:02,257
I've come here to find out why a killer

1223
00:41:02,257 --> 00:41:04,113
can terrorize an entire city,

1224
00:41:04,113 --> 00:41:06,460
while the police department
twiddles its thumbs.

1225
00:41:06,460 --> 00:41:08,508
- Oh, well, now, I wouldn't exactly call

1226
00:41:08,508 --> 00:41:10,033
what we were doing "thumb
twiddling", Commissioner.

1227
00:41:10,033 --> 00:41:11,335
- [Mike] Rochester.

1228
00:41:11,335 --> 00:41:12,657
- [Parkington] Then just what
would you call it, Captain?

1229
00:41:12,657 --> 00:41:13,681
- [Tom] Thumb wrestling.

1230
00:41:13,681 --> 00:41:15,121
- His honor, the mayor,
would like to know.

1231
00:41:15,121 --> 00:41:16,657
- Now if you gentlemen
will please sit down,

1232
00:41:16,657 --> 00:41:18,535
I'll try to bring you up
to date on the creeper.

1233
00:41:18,535 --> 00:41:20,113
- We are up to date on the creeper.

1234
00:41:20,113 --> 00:41:21,948
We know that you haven't arrested him,

1235
00:41:21,948 --> 00:41:23,484
and his honor, the mayor,
delivered an ultimatim

1236
00:41:23,484 --> 00:41:25,711
to Commissioner Salisbury this morning.

1237
00:41:25,711 --> 00:41:28,452
He demanded action within 24 hours.

1238
00:41:28,452 --> 00:41:30,169
If none is forthcoming, he will ask

1239
00:41:30,169 --> 00:41:31,727
for the commissioner's resignation.

1240
00:41:31,727 --> 00:41:33,465
- And I'm telling you,
Donelly, if you don't

1241
00:41:33,465 --> 00:41:35,449
turn up something to quiet public opinion

1242
00:41:35,449 --> 00:41:38,159
before tomorrow, your job
won't be worth a pumpernickel.

1243
00:41:38,159 --> 00:41:39,044
- [Crow] Get his puffer!

1244
00:41:39,044 --> 00:41:40,345
- Exactly what I was
telling Lieutenant Gates

1245
00:41:40,345 --> 00:41:43,065
when you came in, wasn't I, Lieutenant?

1246
00:41:43,065 --> 00:41:46,084
- Yes, sir, you were,
and I'll pass the word

1247
00:41:46,084 --> 00:41:47,788
right on down the line until every man

1248
00:41:47,788 --> 00:41:50,017
in the department knows exactly
where this thing stands.

1249
00:41:50,017 --> 00:41:52,300
You can depend on me, Gentemen.

1250
00:41:52,300 --> 00:41:53,292
- Very well.

1251
00:41:53,292 --> 00:41:55,936
Shall we go, Commissioner?

1252
00:41:55,936 --> 00:41:58,420
- [Crow and Tom] We
represent the Lollipop Guild,

1253
00:41:58,420 --> 00:42:00,703
the Lollipop Guild.

1254
00:42:00,703 --> 00:42:01,536
- How'd I do?

1255
00:42:01,536 --> 00:42:02,879
- Great, we're still a good team.

1256
00:42:02,879 --> 00:42:04,137
- [Tom] That was a long, dull scene.

1257
00:42:04,137 --> 00:42:05,599
(phone rings)
Congratulations.

1258
00:42:05,599 --> 00:42:07,988
- Homicide, Captain Donelly speaking.

1259
00:42:07,988 --> 00:42:11,049
- [Mike] No, you're
looking for armed robbery.

1260
00:42:11,049 --> 00:42:13,844
- Okay, okay, we'll look into it.

1261
00:42:13,844 --> 00:42:14,677
(phone clicks)

1262
00:42:14,677 --> 00:42:15,796
Everybody in town's got creeper-itis.

1263
00:42:15,796 --> 00:42:16,629
- Who was it?

1264
00:42:16,629 --> 00:42:18,303
- Some grocer down near the waterfront

1265
00:42:18,303 --> 00:42:19,647
thinks the creeper might have knocked off

1266
00:42:19,647 --> 00:42:20,649
his delivery boy.

1267
00:42:20,649 --> 00:42:21,482
- [Crow] So?

1268
00:42:21,482 --> 00:42:23,209
Creeper, creeper, creeper.

1269
00:42:23,209 --> 00:42:25,897
- We just have time to go
down there before lunch.

1270
00:42:25,897 --> 00:42:29,314
(upbeat synth pop music)

1271
00:42:31,935 --> 00:42:33,097
- Whoa, whoa, whoa, how long you gonna be

1272
00:42:33,097 --> 00:42:34,740
on that phone, there,
Tex, because I'm expecting

1273
00:42:34,740 --> 00:42:36,553
an important phone call
from Sheri, my realtor,

1274
00:42:36,553 --> 00:42:38,153
about the duplex and all.

1275
00:42:38,153 --> 00:42:39,391
- Yeah, that's great, but listen, Tom,

1276
00:42:39,391 --> 00:42:41,407
I had the idea, hey, we have a phone.

1277
00:42:41,407 --> 00:42:44,329
Why not call someone like
my old girlfriend, Karla,

1278
00:42:44,329 --> 00:42:46,260
and she could help us get down from here.

1279
00:42:46,260 --> 00:42:47,679
- Oh, that's a cute idea, too, Mike,

1280
00:42:47,679 --> 00:42:48,905
but keep it around 30 seconds, will you,

1281
00:42:48,905 --> 00:42:50,836
because Sheri's probably
trying to get a hold of me.

1282
00:42:50,836 --> 00:42:52,020
(phone dialing)
- Yeah, whatever, it's fine.

1283
00:42:52,020 --> 00:42:53,044
- [Voiceover] Hello?

1284
00:42:53,044 --> 00:42:54,153
- Oh, hi, Karla.

1285
00:42:54,153 --> 00:42:56,259
Hey it's me, Mike Nelson.

1286
00:42:56,259 --> 00:42:57,092
- [Voiceover] Mike!

1287
00:42:57,092 --> 00:42:58,153
Hi!

1288
00:42:58,153 --> 00:42:59,380
How are you?

1289
00:42:59,380 --> 00:43:00,213
- I'm good.

1290
00:43:00,213 --> 00:43:02,655
Hey, listen very carefully,
this is gonna sound weird,

1291
00:43:02,655 --> 00:43:03,977
but I'm trapped up in space--

1292
00:43:03,977 --> 00:43:05,396
- [Voiceover] Oh, hang on, Mike,

1293
00:43:05,396 --> 00:43:07,784
my little boy, Matthew,
wants to say "hi" to you.

1294
00:43:07,784 --> 00:43:08,617
- Well, no, this is kind of--

1295
00:43:08,617 --> 00:43:09,756
- [Voiceover] Go ahead, Matthew.

1296
00:43:09,756 --> 00:43:10,589
(heavy breathing)

1297
00:43:10,589 --> 00:43:11,612
Say "hi" to Mike.

1298
00:43:11,612 --> 00:43:12,612
- Hi, Matt.

1299
00:43:12,612 --> 00:43:13,935
- [Voiceover] Say, "Hi, Mike."

1300
00:43:13,935 --> 00:43:14,768
- Hi.

1301
00:43:14,768 --> 00:43:15,833
- [Voiceover] Say, "Hi, Mike."

1302
00:43:15,833 --> 00:43:16,676
- Hi.

1303
00:43:16,676 --> 00:43:17,604
- [Voiceover] Hi.

1304
00:43:17,604 --> 00:43:21,412
- Hi, could you give the
phone back now, please, Matt?

1305
00:43:21,412 --> 00:43:24,333
(high voice babbling)

1306
00:43:24,333 --> 00:43:26,965
That's great, Matt, that's terrific,

1307
00:43:26,965 --> 00:43:27,798
sounds like fun.

1308
00:43:27,798 --> 00:43:29,866
Hey, could you give the
phone back to your mom, now?

1309
00:43:29,866 --> 00:43:31,978
(high voice babbling)

1310
00:43:31,978 --> 00:43:32,811
Great, uh-huh.

1311
00:43:32,811 --> 00:43:34,016
Give the phone back, Matt!

1312
00:43:34,016 --> 00:43:35,114
- Mike, let me take care of this,

1313
00:43:35,114 --> 00:43:36,245
let me take care of this.

1314
00:43:36,245 --> 00:43:37,130
Matt, can you hear me?

1315
00:43:37,130 --> 00:43:37,963
- [Voiceover] Yeah.

1316
00:43:37,963 --> 00:43:38,986
- Listen, Son, hang up the phone.

1317
00:43:38,986 --> 00:43:40,523
I'm trying to get a hold
of my realtor, Sheri, okay?

1318
00:43:40,523 --> 00:43:41,408
- [Voiceover] Okay, bye.

1319
00:43:41,408 --> 00:43:42,400
- No, don't hang up, Matt!

1320
00:43:42,400 --> 00:43:43,466
(dial tone droning)
Now look what you've done!

1321
00:43:43,466 --> 00:43:44,768
This was our chance to get down.

1322
00:43:44,768 --> 00:43:45,813
(alarm buzzing)

1323
00:43:45,813 --> 00:43:46,774
- What'd I do?

1324
00:43:46,774 --> 00:43:48,117
It's only my realtor, for crying out loud.

1325
00:43:48,117 --> 00:43:49,322
(engine whirring)

1326
00:43:49,322 --> 00:43:51,786
(metal scraping)

1327
00:43:51,786 --> 00:43:53,258
(door slams)

1328
00:43:53,258 --> 00:43:54,346
(chains clinking)
(liquid dripping)

1329
00:43:54,346 --> 00:43:56,522
(door sliding)
(beeping)

1330
00:43:56,522 --> 00:43:59,893
(bolts clanking)

1331
00:43:59,893 --> 00:44:02,060
- [Crow] Creeper, creeper.

1332
00:44:03,605 --> 00:44:05,740
- [Tom] Oh, I do the best stir fry.

1333
00:44:05,740 --> 00:44:06,760
You're in for a treat, my little

1334
00:44:06,760 --> 00:44:08,238
delivery-boy friend.

1335
00:44:08,238 --> 00:44:09,435
- [Mike] Hey, wake up, sunshine.

1336
00:44:09,435 --> 00:44:12,693
- [Crow] Oh, he used the
last set of clean sheets.

1337
00:44:12,693 --> 00:44:15,270
(ominous orchestral music)
(engine rumbling)

1338
00:44:15,270 --> 00:44:17,808
- [Tom] Oh, no, the apaches.

1339
00:44:17,808 --> 00:44:18,723
(engine rumbling)

1340
00:44:18,723 --> 00:44:20,941
- [Mike] This is a noisy apartment.

1341
00:44:20,941 --> 00:44:22,501
- [Lieutenant] Here's number 23, Donelly.

1342
00:44:22,501 --> 00:44:26,056
- [Crow] Be quiet, we
have to sneak up on him.

1343
00:44:26,056 --> 00:44:27,513
(water lapping)

1344
00:44:27,513 --> 00:44:29,623
- [Tom] So, I got time to snag a sturgeon?

1345
00:44:29,623 --> 00:44:30,984
- [Crow] Come on, here, boy.

1346
00:44:30,984 --> 00:44:32,034
Heel.

1347
00:44:32,034 --> 00:44:35,760
(ominous orchestral music)

1348
00:44:35,760 --> 00:44:38,095
- [Mike] Oh, I forgot about
the realtor coming over.

1349
00:44:38,095 --> 00:44:39,679
- [Crow] I'll just push
the body under the rug,

1350
00:44:39,679 --> 00:44:40,899
and give it a lick and promise.

1351
00:44:40,899 --> 00:44:44,067
- [Tom] Hate to leave my boards behind.

1352
00:44:44,067 --> 00:44:46,367
(footsteps)

1353
00:44:46,367 --> 00:44:48,714
- [Mike] I door-knocked out
here for Wellstone, once.

1354
00:44:48,714 --> 00:44:50,685
- [Crow] Hey, it's Katie May.

1355
00:44:50,685 --> 00:44:52,726
(door handle rattling)

1356
00:44:52,726 --> 00:44:54,205
- [Tom] Hello?

1357
00:44:54,205 --> 00:44:55,179
(doorknob rattling)

1358
00:44:55,179 --> 00:44:57,488
- [Donelly] Come on, open up in there.

1359
00:44:57,488 --> 00:44:59,536
- [Crow] Oh no.

1360
00:44:59,536 --> 00:45:01,512
(door breaking)

1361
00:45:01,512 --> 00:45:02,662
- [Mike] Beep, beep.

1362
00:45:02,662 --> 00:45:05,548
Alert, intruder, alert, intruder.

1363
00:45:05,548 --> 00:45:09,298
- [Crow] Hmm, what a
really good plant house.

1364
00:45:10,195 --> 00:45:11,626
- [Tom] The crawl space is a little wet,

1365
00:45:11,626 --> 00:45:13,626
but there's a sump pump.

1366
00:45:15,903 --> 00:45:18,561
- [Mike] I hate these shared bathrooms.

1367
00:45:18,561 --> 00:45:21,212
(ominous orchestral music)

1368
00:45:21,212 --> 00:45:22,586
- Look.

1369
00:45:22,586 --> 00:45:23,501
- [Crow] Can it wait?

1370
00:45:23,501 --> 00:45:24,834
I'm fixing eggs.

1371
00:45:26,646 --> 00:45:29,122
- [Crow] Does this body mean anything?

1372
00:45:29,122 --> 00:45:31,455
(footsteps)

1373
00:45:32,395 --> 00:45:33,487
- [Mike] Okay, I looked.

1374
00:45:33,487 --> 00:45:34,320
Happy?

1375
00:45:34,320 --> 00:45:35,528
- The creeper again.

1376
00:45:35,528 --> 00:45:36,361
- Yep.

1377
00:45:37,863 --> 00:45:39,189
From the looks of that coffee pot,

1378
00:45:39,189 --> 00:45:40,714
he can't be far away.

1379
00:45:40,714 --> 00:45:41,970
- [Tom] He never leaves half a pot.

1380
00:45:41,970 --> 00:45:43,248
- Round up some men and start
combing the neighborhood

1381
00:45:43,248 --> 00:45:44,101
and I'll have a look around here.

1382
00:45:44,101 --> 00:45:44,934
- Okay.

1383
00:45:46,319 --> 00:45:48,008
- [Crow] Where are those filters?

1384
00:45:48,008 --> 00:45:49,639
- [Mike] Oh, god, the creepers bedpan.

1385
00:45:49,639 --> 00:45:51,722
- [Tom and Crow] Oh, eww.

1386
00:45:52,861 --> 00:45:54,884
(footsteps)

1387
00:45:54,884 --> 00:45:55,717
- [Tom] Well, that does it.

1388
00:45:55,717 --> 00:45:58,050
My investigation's complete.

1389
00:45:59,815 --> 00:46:01,410
- [Crow] Say, you're one good-looking man.

1390
00:46:01,410 --> 00:46:02,269
Who loves you, baby?

1391
00:46:02,269 --> 00:46:03,602
Oh, a zit there.

1392
00:46:04,551 --> 00:46:05,384
(door opens)

1393
00:46:05,384 --> 00:46:07,019
- [Mike] Hi, guy.

1394
00:46:07,019 --> 00:46:09,239
- [Tom] Look at all
these Lanecome products.

1395
00:46:09,239 --> 00:46:11,989
(paper rustling)

1396
00:46:13,704 --> 00:46:17,371
- [Crow] These three saw
Popular Trio, recently.

1397
00:46:17,371 --> 00:46:21,297
- [Tom] Popular Trio
still under investigation.

1398
00:46:21,297 --> 00:46:23,055
- [Mike] Oh, man, yeah, they were popular.

1399
00:46:23,055 --> 00:46:25,593
So full of life, it was
great to be around them.

1400
00:46:25,593 --> 00:46:28,176
Well, I'll look for more clues.

1401
00:46:29,358 --> 00:46:31,922
- [Crow] Oh, he sure is into coupons.

1402
00:46:31,922 --> 00:46:34,265
- [Mike] Look at this, he put
applesauce in his meatloaf.

1403
00:46:34,265 --> 00:46:36,669
That's a terrific idea.

1404
00:46:36,669 --> 00:46:40,570
- [Tom] Yep, sure is creeping, there.

1405
00:46:40,570 --> 00:46:42,177
- [Crow] Oh, damn, where's that northern?

1406
00:46:42,177 --> 00:46:44,765
I had it chained up right here.

1407
00:46:44,765 --> 00:46:46,102
(footsteps)

1408
00:46:46,102 --> 00:46:49,648
- [Mike] No, I won't talk to you anymore.

1409
00:46:49,648 --> 00:46:50,617
(water swishing)

1410
00:46:50,617 --> 00:46:53,094
- [Tom] Well, looks
like we combed the area.

1411
00:46:53,094 --> 00:46:56,112
(laughing)

1412
00:46:56,112 --> 00:46:57,682
- I'm going to call
headquarters for more men.

1413
00:46:57,682 --> 00:46:59,498
We'll give the whole district
a house to house canvas.

1414
00:46:59,498 --> 00:47:01,424
- [Crow] That'll take a
very large canvas, sir.

1415
00:47:01,424 --> 00:47:03,828
- [Tom] Say, anybody know
where the AJ Fiske and Son

1416
00:47:03,828 --> 00:47:05,411
Sausage Company is?

1417
00:47:07,091 --> 00:47:09,888
And who is walking on my bridge?

1418
00:47:09,888 --> 00:47:11,470
(gasp)

1419
00:47:11,470 --> 00:47:12,303
- [Tom] Boss delighted.

1420
00:47:12,303 --> 00:47:16,361
- [Crow] "Where is olive
loaf?", people demand.

1421
00:47:16,361 --> 00:47:20,389
(scary orchestral music)

1422
00:47:20,389 --> 00:47:21,752
- [Mike] Boy, you know,
murder clues may lead

1423
00:47:21,752 --> 00:47:22,785
to creeper.

1424
00:47:22,785 --> 00:47:26,057
(ominous orchestral music)

1425
00:47:26,057 --> 00:47:29,360
- [Tom] Meanwhile, the thin
man arrives at the White House,

1426
00:47:29,360 --> 00:47:30,693
and his brother.

1427
00:47:34,549 --> 00:47:35,922
- [Crow] Nice knockers, huh?

1428
00:47:35,922 --> 00:47:39,150
- [Mike] Love that joke.
(laughing)

1429
00:47:39,150 --> 00:47:40,469
(knocking)

1430
00:47:40,469 --> 00:47:41,523
- [Crow] Trick or treat for UNICEF.

1431
00:47:41,523 --> 00:47:42,356
(door opens)

1432
00:47:42,356 --> 00:47:43,189
- Good evening.

1433
00:47:43,189 --> 00:47:44,022
- Good evening.

1434
00:47:44,022 --> 00:47:44,855
- Are you Mr. Clifford Scott?

1435
00:47:44,855 --> 00:47:45,688
- Yes.

1436
00:47:45,688 --> 00:47:47,111
- Captain Donelly, Homicide Burrow,

1437
00:47:47,111 --> 00:47:47,944
and Lieutenant Gates.

1438
00:47:47,944 --> 00:47:49,186
Mind if we come in?

1439
00:47:49,186 --> 00:47:50,510
- Not at all, come right in.

1440
00:47:50,510 --> 00:47:51,578
- Thank you.

1441
00:47:51,578 --> 00:47:52,761
- [Mike] We were wondering why you weren't

1442
00:47:52,761 --> 00:47:53,594
wearing a hat.

1443
00:47:53,594 --> 00:47:54,427
(door closes)

1444
00:47:54,427 --> 00:47:55,481
- [Tom] Tom Dewey, at home.

1445
00:47:55,481 --> 00:47:56,658
- What can I do for you gentlemen?

1446
00:47:56,658 --> 00:47:58,474
- I believe you can give us
some important information

1447
00:47:58,474 --> 00:47:59,566
if you will.

1448
00:47:59,566 --> 00:48:00,792
- I don't know what this is all about

1449
00:48:00,792 --> 00:48:02,473
but I'll be glad to help
in any way that I can.

1450
00:48:02,473 --> 00:48:03,491
Shall we go into the living room?

1451
00:48:03,491 --> 00:48:04,324
- Thank you.

1452
00:48:04,324 --> 00:48:06,055
- [Tom] It's just this way about a mile.

1453
00:48:06,055 --> 00:48:07,232
- What is it, Clifford?

1454
00:48:07,232 --> 00:48:08,508
- I don't know yet, dear.

1455
00:48:08,508 --> 00:48:09,980
These gentlemen are from
the police department.

1456
00:48:09,980 --> 00:48:11,526
They say they'd like to
ask me some questions.

1457
00:48:11,526 --> 00:48:12,777
Captain Donelly,
Lieutenant Gates, my wife.

1458
00:48:12,777 --> 00:48:13,610
- How do you do?

1459
00:48:13,610 --> 00:48:15,267
- You can help us, too, Mrs. Scott.

1460
00:48:15,267 --> 00:48:16,100
- I?

1461
00:48:16,100 --> 00:48:16,933
- Yes.

1462
00:48:16,933 --> 00:48:18,039
I take it you've read about the murders

1463
00:48:18,039 --> 00:48:19,671
committed by a character
the newspapers call

1464
00:48:19,671 --> 00:48:20,787
"the creeper"?

1465
00:48:20,787 --> 00:48:22,026
- Why, yes.

1466
00:48:22,026 --> 00:48:23,805
- We stumbled onto something this morning.

1467
00:48:23,805 --> 00:48:24,884
- [Crow] We stubbed our toe.

1468
00:48:24,884 --> 00:48:26,111
- This leads me to believe you can

1469
00:48:26,111 --> 00:48:27,313
help us identify him.

1470
00:48:27,313 --> 00:48:28,834
- You mean it's someone we know?

1471
00:48:28,834 --> 00:48:30,111
- I think so.

1472
00:48:30,111 --> 00:48:32,330
What was your class at Hampton University?

1473
00:48:32,330 --> 00:48:33,667
- 1930.

1474
00:48:33,667 --> 00:48:35,556
- You were in that class,
too, weren't you, Mrs. Scott?

1475
00:48:35,556 --> 00:48:36,439
- Yes.

1476
00:48:36,439 --> 00:48:38,868
- And you had a classmate
named Hal Moffat?

1477
00:48:38,868 --> 00:48:39,800
Remember him?

1478
00:48:39,800 --> 00:48:40,818
- He was a good friend of ours.

1479
00:48:40,818 --> 00:48:42,180
- [Mike] He sat on a tuffet.

1480
00:48:42,180 --> 00:48:44,693
- We have an idea that
Hal Moffat is the creeper.

1481
00:48:44,693 --> 00:48:45,627
- Hal?

1482
00:48:45,627 --> 00:48:47,129
- No, he couldn't be.

1483
00:48:47,129 --> 00:48:48,883
- We found this in his shack, among some

1484
00:48:48,883 --> 00:48:50,539
of this other things.

1485
00:48:50,539 --> 00:48:52,722
Apparently, he'd been
keeping it for some time.

1486
00:48:52,722 --> 00:48:53,618
- [Tom] Let me see here.

1487
00:48:53,618 --> 00:48:55,141
I'm Hal Moffet, I'm the creeper.

1488
00:48:55,141 --> 00:48:55,974
What do you know?

1489
00:48:55,974 --> 00:48:56,807
(paper rustling)

1490
00:48:56,807 --> 00:48:58,009
- [Clifford] I remember
when this was taken.

1491
00:48:58,009 --> 00:48:59,455
Was right after a football game.

1492
00:48:59,455 --> 00:49:02,962
- Yes, it was the day Hal almost
won the game single-handed.

1493
00:49:02,962 --> 00:49:06,060
- Two people from that college
were killed by the creeper.

1494
00:49:06,060 --> 00:49:09,797
Professor Cushman and a
woman named Joan Bemis.

1495
00:49:09,797 --> 00:49:11,129
- We knew Joan quite well.

1496
00:49:11,129 --> 00:49:12,639
- I thought you might of.

1497
00:49:12,639 --> 00:49:14,482
When did you last see Hal Moffat?

1498
00:49:14,482 --> 00:49:15,801
- [Mike] He was eating curds and whey.

1499
00:49:15,801 --> 00:49:17,055
- We haven't seen him for years.

1500
00:49:17,055 --> 00:49:18,809
- No, he just disappeared.

1501
00:49:18,809 --> 00:49:19,948
- [Donelly] Disappeared?

1502
00:49:19,948 --> 00:49:21,317
- Well, none of us has seen him since

1503
00:49:21,317 --> 00:49:22,930
his last year in college.

1504
00:49:22,930 --> 00:49:25,042
You see, something happened to Hal.

1505
00:49:25,042 --> 00:49:25,887
- [Donelly] What?

1506
00:49:25,887 --> 00:49:26,720
- [Crow] He graduated.

1507
00:49:26,720 --> 00:49:28,613
- He was probably our best friend.

1508
00:49:28,613 --> 00:49:29,753
- [Mike] If I'm thinking of the right guy.

1509
00:49:29,753 --> 00:49:31,545
- His last year at Hampton, he was captain

1510
00:49:31,545 --> 00:49:32,927
of the football team.

1511
00:49:32,927 --> 00:49:33,760
- [Tom] Here we go.

1512
00:49:33,760 --> 00:49:34,655
- [Clifford] In the
final game of the season,

1513
00:49:34,655 --> 00:49:37,074
we were playing Rensler for
the Compton's championship.

1514
00:49:37,074 --> 00:49:39,801
Sports writers picked Hal
for All-American honors.

1515
00:49:39,801 --> 00:49:42,053
If they had any doubt about
it, the game he played

1516
00:49:42,053 --> 00:49:43,679
against Princeton won their votes.

1517
00:49:43,679 --> 00:49:44,512
- [Crow] Harpo!

1518
00:49:44,512 --> 00:49:46,533
- [Clifford] Hal had a quick
temper, but it certainly

1519
00:49:46,533 --> 00:49:47,954
paid off on the football field.

1520
00:49:47,954 --> 00:49:49,068
- [Crow] Sieg, Heil!

1521
00:49:49,068 --> 00:49:49,901
- [Clifford] He was all over the place,

1522
00:49:49,901 --> 00:49:51,744
blocking, backing up his
line, passing so much

1523
00:49:51,744 --> 00:49:54,034
we were dizzy, and up
to the fourth quarter,

1524
00:49:54,034 --> 00:49:55,903
had scored three touchdowns
and brilliant broken

1525
00:49:55,903 --> 00:49:56,991
field runs.

1526
00:49:56,991 --> 00:49:58,553
- [Tom] 14 RBIs and three assists.

1527
00:49:58,553 --> 00:50:00,613
- [Clifford] Three times he
converted for extra points.

1528
00:50:00,613 --> 00:50:04,197
Score stood: Hampton, 21, Rensler, seven.

1529
00:50:04,197 --> 00:50:05,322
- [Mike] Okay, great, right there.

1530
00:50:05,322 --> 00:50:07,082
Don't move it, no, you moved it, no.

1531
00:50:07,082 --> 00:50:08,079
(crowd cheering)

1532
00:50:08,079 --> 00:50:09,040
- [Crow] Ah, hang on, I'm having a private

1533
00:50:09,040 --> 00:50:12,189
flashback right now, I'll get back to you.

1534
00:50:12,189 --> 00:50:15,248
(whistling)
(yelling)

1535
00:50:15,248 --> 00:50:17,514
- [Clifford] Hal's temper
made him a lot of enemies,

1536
00:50:17,514 --> 00:50:19,575
but this year, everyone was his friend.

1537
00:50:19,575 --> 00:50:21,687
He was one of the best
players Hampton ever produced

1538
00:50:21,687 --> 00:50:23,645
and we were proud of him.

1539
00:50:23,645 --> 00:50:24,960
- [Tom] Ah, sir, this is really neat,

1540
00:50:24,960 --> 00:50:25,847
(gun firing)
but could we get back to

1541
00:50:25,847 --> 00:50:26,928
the murder case?

1542
00:50:26,928 --> 00:50:27,809
(crowd cheering)

1543
00:50:27,809 --> 00:50:29,290
- [Clifford] Hal and I
were very close friends,

1544
00:50:29,290 --> 00:50:31,043
and there was only one catch to it.

1545
00:50:31,043 --> 00:50:32,989
We were both in love with Virginia,

1546
00:50:32,989 --> 00:50:35,114
and to complicate matters, Joan Bemis

1547
00:50:35,114 --> 00:50:36,586
was madly in love with Hal.

1548
00:50:36,586 --> 00:50:37,789
- [Crow] A football.

1549
00:50:37,789 --> 00:50:39,786
- [Clifford] We were going
to celebrate that night,

1550
00:50:39,786 --> 00:50:40,631
the four of us.

1551
00:50:40,631 --> 00:50:42,192
- [Mike] We each had patty melts.

1552
00:50:42,192 --> 00:50:43,549
- [Clifford] I had a date with Virginia,

1553
00:50:43,549 --> 00:50:45,674
but Hal did a little conniving.

1554
00:50:45,674 --> 00:50:46,954
He picked her up in his car and didn't

1555
00:50:46,954 --> 00:50:48,899
arrive until the evening was half over,

1556
00:50:48,899 --> 00:50:50,551
leaving Joan and me to amuse ourselves

1557
00:50:50,551 --> 00:50:52,803
by staring at each other across the table.

1558
00:50:52,803 --> 00:50:53,725
- [Tom] Ridiculous, huh?

1559
00:50:53,725 --> 00:50:55,095
- [Clifford] Of course,
he had a story ready

1560
00:50:55,095 --> 00:50:56,567
when they did get there.

1561
00:50:56,567 --> 00:50:58,154
He always had a story,

1562
00:50:58,154 --> 00:51:00,970
and knowing his temper,
I seldom challenged him.

1563
00:51:00,970 --> 00:51:01,891
- Hi.

1564
00:51:01,891 --> 00:51:02,979
- [Crow] Well, what do you mean by that?

1565
00:51:02,979 --> 00:51:04,336
- I'm sorry we're late, folks, but I had

1566
00:51:04,336 --> 00:51:05,616
some car trouble and couldn't seem

1567
00:51:05,616 --> 00:51:07,203
to find out what it was.

1568
00:51:07,203 --> 00:51:09,328
All of a sudden, it
started, just like that.

1569
00:51:09,328 --> 00:51:10,723
You know how it is, Cliff, old boy.

1570
00:51:10,723 --> 00:51:13,117
- [Clifford] Hal did the scoring again,

1571
00:51:13,117 --> 00:51:15,677
just like he did on the
field that afternoon.

1572
00:51:15,677 --> 00:51:17,328
- [Crow] So tell me about this cat poster,

1573
00:51:17,328 --> 00:51:19,389
apparently, the cat's hanging on a stick.

1574
00:51:19,389 --> 00:51:21,118
- [Mike] Jag off, jerk.

1575
00:51:21,118 --> 00:51:22,730
- [Clifford] Hal and I were roommates.

1576
00:51:22,730 --> 00:51:24,573
He was the athlete and I was a scholar.

1577
00:51:24,573 --> 00:51:25,763
- [Crow] Let's make lots of money.

1578
00:51:25,763 --> 00:51:27,888
- [Clifford] I used to help
him a lot with his studies.

1579
00:51:27,888 --> 00:51:30,119
It's an old college custom,
keeping football stars

1580
00:51:30,119 --> 00:51:31,943
eligible to play.

1581
00:51:31,943 --> 00:51:33,504
It wasn't because Hal was dumb.

1582
00:51:33,504 --> 00:51:34,337
- [Tom] But he was.

1583
00:51:34,337 --> 00:51:36,560
- [Clifford] He was just
to impatient to study hard.

1584
00:51:36,560 --> 00:51:38,722
On the football, he got action quick,

1585
00:51:38,722 --> 00:51:40,410
but you can't stiff-arm
your way through a flock

1586
00:51:40,410 --> 00:51:42,368
of chemistry problems.

1587
00:51:42,368 --> 00:51:44,096
- Yep, I got a very
heavy date with Virginia

1588
00:51:44,096 --> 00:51:46,016
right after chemistry class, and boy,

1589
00:51:46,016 --> 00:51:47,114
have I got plans.

1590
00:51:47,114 --> 00:51:48,087
- [Tom] Did I ask?

1591
00:51:48,087 --> 00:51:49,674
- [Clifford] Hal did some boasting,

1592
00:51:49,674 --> 00:51:51,235
and I did some thinking.

1593
00:51:51,235 --> 00:51:52,490
Then he made the mistake of asking me

1594
00:51:52,490 --> 00:51:54,525
to check his chemistry answers.

1595
00:51:54,525 --> 00:51:56,087
I saw a chance to keep him from having

1596
00:51:56,087 --> 00:51:57,866
that date with Virginia.

1597
00:51:57,866 --> 00:52:00,759
His answers were right, but I fixed that.

1598
00:52:00,759 --> 00:52:02,960
I gave him a set of wrong ones, and he

1599
00:52:02,960 --> 00:52:04,445
memorized them very carefully for the

1600
00:52:04,445 --> 00:52:06,480
verbal quiz scheduled for the next day.

1601
00:52:06,480 --> 00:52:08,042
- [Mike] So you're a big fat jerk.

1602
00:52:08,042 --> 00:52:09,847
- Hydrochloric acid, sulfuric acid,

1603
00:52:09,847 --> 00:52:13,495
and nitric acid are all catalytic agents.

1604
00:52:13,495 --> 00:52:14,672
- Very good, Mr. Scott.

1605
00:52:14,672 --> 00:52:16,093
- [Tom] But I didn't ask.

1606
00:52:16,093 --> 00:52:17,117
- Mr. Moffat.

1607
00:52:17,117 --> 00:52:18,563
- [Crow] Sit on my tuffet.

1608
00:52:18,563 --> 00:52:19,690
(desk clunks)

1609
00:52:19,690 --> 00:52:21,994
- Sulfuric acid, when combined
with H four and seven,

1610
00:52:21,994 --> 00:52:24,835
will form a solution of hydrochloric acid.

1611
00:52:24,835 --> 00:52:26,692
- [Mike] Mr. Moffat, I have never spanked

1612
00:52:26,692 --> 00:52:29,043
a senior before until now.

1613
00:52:29,043 --> 00:52:31,923
- You better remain
after class, Mr. Moffat.

1614
00:52:31,923 --> 00:52:34,599
You seem to have everything wrong today.

1615
00:52:34,599 --> 00:52:35,943
Class dismissed.

1616
00:52:35,943 --> 00:52:37,632
- [Tom] He'll never suspect me.

1617
00:52:37,632 --> 00:52:40,039
- [Clifford] Hal realized
that I'd crossed him up.

1618
00:52:40,039 --> 00:52:41,600
He was accustomed to being a winner,

1619
00:52:41,600 --> 00:52:43,149
never a loser.

1620
00:52:43,149 --> 00:52:44,967
He was plenty angry
because I'd outsmarted him

1621
00:52:44,967 --> 00:52:46,336
and he showed it.

1622
00:52:46,336 --> 00:52:49,661
- [Crow] I'm gonna grow
an ugly face and kill you.

1623
00:52:49,661 --> 00:52:51,238
(ominous orchestral music)

1624
00:52:51,238 --> 00:52:52,403
- [Clifford] Professor
Cushman put Hal to work

1625
00:52:52,403 --> 00:52:54,131
on a difficult experiment
that would keep him busy

1626
00:52:54,131 --> 00:52:56,167
the rest of the afternoon.

1627
00:52:56,167 --> 00:52:57,997
And just to needle him, I walked Virginia

1628
00:52:57,997 --> 00:52:59,392
past the laboratory window.

1629
00:52:59,392 --> 00:53:00,414
- [Mike] Oh, you're a good friend.

1630
00:53:00,414 --> 00:53:01,850
- [Clifford] The look he gave us told me

1631
00:53:01,850 --> 00:53:03,600
I'd better get away from there quick.

1632
00:53:03,600 --> 00:53:04,880
You could never tell what he'd do

1633
00:53:04,880 --> 00:53:05,751
when his temper flared.

1634
00:53:05,751 --> 00:53:06,954
- [Crow] Come on, let's
go throw cigarette butts

1635
00:53:06,954 --> 00:53:08,205
at the monkeys.

1636
00:53:08,205 --> 00:53:10,363
(orchestral swell)

1637
00:53:10,363 --> 00:53:11,617
(glass shattering)

1638
00:53:11,617 --> 00:53:12,776
- [Crow] Ha!

1639
00:53:12,776 --> 00:53:14,389
- [Mike] Out, vile jelly.

1640
00:53:14,389 --> 00:53:15,246
- [Tom] Am I okay?

1641
00:53:15,246 --> 00:53:17,512
Yeah, I think I am.

1642
00:53:17,512 --> 00:53:19,150
- [Clifford] This time,
it brought tragedy.

1643
00:53:19,150 --> 00:53:20,353
- [Crow] Do you want the lab notes?

1644
00:53:20,353 --> 00:53:21,941
- [Clifford] We visited
him in the hospital,

1645
00:53:21,941 --> 00:53:25,401
and tried to talk to him,
but he wouldn't answer.

1646
00:53:25,401 --> 00:53:28,662
He just lay there, staring up at us.

1647
00:53:28,662 --> 00:53:30,209
The doctors told us
that the chemicals might

1648
00:53:30,209 --> 00:53:33,601
affect certain glands and
nerves, and if they did,

1649
00:53:33,601 --> 00:53:36,136
his features would never be normal again.

1650
00:53:36,136 --> 00:53:37,697
- [Donelly] And you hadn't
seen him since then?

1651
00:53:37,697 --> 00:53:39,489
- No, he left the hospital
after several weeks

1652
00:53:39,489 --> 00:53:41,320
and just dropped out of sight.

1653
00:53:41,320 --> 00:53:42,933
- Did the doctors tell you
anything else about him,

1654
00:53:42,933 --> 00:53:44,609
I mean, about his mental state?

1655
00:53:44,609 --> 00:53:45,442
- Oh, yes.

1656
00:53:45,442 --> 00:53:46,531
- [Tom] He was mental.

1657
00:53:46,531 --> 00:53:47,745
- One of them did say
that Hal was pretty bitter

1658
00:53:47,745 --> 00:53:48,578
when he left.

1659
00:53:48,578 --> 00:53:49,411
- Yeah.

1660
00:53:49,411 --> 00:53:50,244
- [Crow] I wonder why.

1661
00:53:50,244 --> 00:53:51,726
- A thing like that could very easily

1662
00:53:51,726 --> 00:53:53,045
have affected his mind.

1663
00:53:53,045 --> 00:53:54,606
And if he is the creeper, I guess

1664
00:53:54,606 --> 00:53:55,912
that's what happened.

1665
00:53:55,912 --> 00:53:57,806
- You mean, he may have
killed Professor Cushman

1666
00:53:57,806 --> 00:54:00,289
just because he kept him
after class that day?

1667
00:54:00,289 --> 00:54:01,877
- Possibly.

1668
00:54:01,877 --> 00:54:02,926
- [Mike] I'm not telling.

1669
00:54:02,926 --> 00:54:04,411
- The mental quirk can develop into an

1670
00:54:04,411 --> 00:54:05,691
extreme case of paranoia.

1671
00:54:05,691 --> 00:54:06,945
- [Tom] So quit looking at me.

1672
00:54:06,945 --> 00:54:08,558
- You say he was in love with you?

1673
00:54:08,558 --> 00:54:10,350
- Well, I don't know.

1674
00:54:10,350 --> 00:54:12,334
I never took Hal very seriously because

1675
00:54:12,334 --> 00:54:14,037
Cliff and I were in love with each other.

1676
00:54:14,037 --> 00:54:15,086
- [Crow] It's over now.

1677
00:54:15,086 --> 00:54:16,264
- I see.

1678
00:54:16,264 --> 00:54:17,761
Well, thank you very
much for the information

1679
00:54:17,761 --> 00:54:18,632
you've given.

1680
00:54:18,632 --> 00:54:19,747
- [Mike] It was long-winded and pointless

1681
00:54:19,747 --> 00:54:21,614
but, thank you.

1682
00:54:21,614 --> 00:54:22,805
(footsteps)

1683
00:54:22,805 --> 00:54:25,544
- [Tom] Say, do you have
a statue I could borrow?

1684
00:54:25,544 --> 00:54:26,900
(door creaking)

1685
00:54:26,900 --> 00:54:28,625
- Oh, I'm going to have your place watched

1686
00:54:28,625 --> 00:54:30,968
day and night for a while, Mr. Scott.

1687
00:54:30,968 --> 00:54:32,862
- You mean, he might come here?

1688
00:54:32,862 --> 00:54:34,334
- You never can tell what a man will do

1689
00:54:34,334 --> 00:54:35,678
when his mind's affected.

1690
00:54:35,678 --> 00:54:37,880
Better to be on the safe side.

1691
00:54:37,880 --> 00:54:40,606
He might hold you responsible
for what happened.

1692
00:54:40,606 --> 00:54:42,923
After all, you gave him the wrong answers.

1693
00:54:42,923 --> 00:54:44,485
- Yes, I did.

1694
00:54:44,485 --> 00:54:45,932
- Well, don't let it upset you.

1695
00:54:45,932 --> 00:54:47,800
We won't let anything happen, and you will

1696
00:54:47,800 --> 00:54:49,643
explain to Mrs. Scott about my men

1697
00:54:49,643 --> 00:54:50,833
covering the place?

1698
00:54:50,833 --> 00:54:51,947
She might think they're prowlers.

1699
00:54:51,947 --> 00:54:52,984
- Surely.

1700
00:54:52,984 --> 00:54:53,817
- Good night.

1701
00:54:53,817 --> 00:54:54,650
- Good night.

1702
00:54:54,650 --> 00:54:56,696
- [Crow] Is there anything I
could have done differently?

1703
00:54:56,696 --> 00:54:57,635
(door closes)

1704
00:54:57,635 --> 00:54:58,822
(cars passing)

1705
00:54:58,822 --> 00:55:01,254
- [Tom] I have often walked
down this street before.

1706
00:55:01,254 --> 00:55:04,646
Feet stay beneath the pavement before.

1707
00:55:04,646 --> 00:55:07,436
On the street where you live.

1708
00:55:07,436 --> 00:55:08,601
- [Mike] I thought I'd look up and it'd

1709
00:55:08,601 --> 00:55:11,110
remind me of something, but no.

1710
00:55:11,110 --> 00:55:14,076
- [Crow] Yes, I'll join band.

1711
00:55:14,076 --> 00:55:17,818
(sad orchestral music)

1712
00:55:17,818 --> 00:55:18,701
- [Tom] Evening, creeper.

1713
00:55:18,701 --> 00:55:20,726
- [Crow] Hi, person.

1714
00:55:20,726 --> 00:55:24,385
(sad orchestral music)

1715
00:55:24,385 --> 00:55:25,910
River wide, oh.

1716
00:55:25,910 --> 00:55:28,950
(sad orchestral music)

1717
00:55:28,950 --> 00:55:31,139
- [Tom] Ah, the Joan Rivers collections.

1718
00:55:31,139 --> 00:55:33,647
- [Mike] Man, how to those
things get off the ground?

1719
00:55:33,647 --> 00:55:36,230
- [Tom] That's a good question.

1720
00:55:37,295 --> 00:55:40,545
- [Crow] I'm going to get a big facial.

1721
00:55:41,468 --> 00:55:45,635
- [Mike] How do you, what
is this, oh, it's a door.

1722
00:55:46,703 --> 00:55:47,715
- Be right with you.

1723
00:55:47,715 --> 00:55:50,298
- [Tom] I gotta lay some cable.

1724
00:55:51,352 --> 00:55:54,527
- [Mike] Have a bra fitter on tonight?

1725
00:55:54,527 --> 00:55:58,110
(ominous orchestral music)

1726
00:55:59,940 --> 00:56:01,412
- See something you like, mister?

1727
00:56:01,412 --> 00:56:05,054
- [Crow] Yeah, but it not on menu.

1728
00:56:05,054 --> 00:56:06,554
- [Tom] I'm looking for a rig to make

1729
00:56:06,554 --> 00:56:08,221
my head look dainty.

1730
00:56:09,089 --> 00:56:10,676
- That's a very fine piece, my friend,

1731
00:56:10,676 --> 00:56:12,327
and very reasonable.

1732
00:56:12,327 --> 00:56:13,940
You can have it for--

1733
00:56:13,940 --> 00:56:16,053
- [Tom] 35 uglies, I mean, dollars!

1734
00:56:16,053 --> 00:56:17,396
- What's the matter?

1735
00:56:17,396 --> 00:56:19,098
- Nothing, I was just
going to say that you

1736
00:56:19,098 --> 00:56:21,261
can buy it for practically nothing.

1737
00:56:21,261 --> 00:56:22,554
- How much?

1738
00:56:22,554 --> 00:56:25,972
- To you, 12 dollars and a half.

1739
00:56:25,972 --> 00:56:27,738
- I'll take it.

1740
00:56:27,738 --> 00:56:29,069
I'll pay you for it tomorrow.

1741
00:56:29,069 --> 00:56:31,796
- Sorry, I don't do business that way.

1742
00:56:31,796 --> 00:56:32,884
- You don't trust me?

1743
00:56:32,884 --> 00:56:35,060
- In my business, it's too expensive.

1744
00:56:35,060 --> 00:56:36,123
- [Tom] Is he Dvorak?

1745
00:56:36,123 --> 00:56:39,245
- Put it back or I'll call the police.

1746
00:56:39,245 --> 00:56:40,269
(grabbing)
(orchestral swell)

1747
00:56:40,269 --> 00:56:41,524
- Help! Help!
- [Crow] Okay, tomorrow's

1748
00:56:41,524 --> 00:56:42,941
fine, fine, fine!

1749
00:56:45,732 --> 00:56:50,237
(car passing)
(ominous orchestral music)

1750
00:56:50,237 --> 00:56:51,275
Evening, creeper.

1751
00:56:51,275 --> 00:56:52,775
- [Tom] I, fellow.

1752
00:56:53,695 --> 00:56:56,767
- [Mike] Wow, the world
sure was dingy back then.

1753
00:56:56,767 --> 00:56:59,533
(singing)

1754
00:56:59,533 --> 00:57:03,333
(ominous orchestral music)

1755
00:57:03,333 --> 00:57:04,533
Just like Toots Thielemans.

1756
00:57:04,533 --> 00:57:06,171
- [Crow] Thank you.

1757
00:57:06,171 --> 00:57:09,796
- [Mike] Rondo Hatton
in Barefoot in the Park.

1758
00:57:09,796 --> 00:57:11,266
- [Tom] I wonder what the
odds are that I live here.

1759
00:57:11,266 --> 00:57:12,099
- [Woman] Who's there?

1760
00:57:12,099 --> 00:57:12,943
- It's me.

1761
00:57:12,943 --> 00:57:14,171
- [Crow] Mr. Creeper.

1762
00:57:14,171 --> 00:57:15,311
- [Woman] Oh, I'm glad.

1763
00:57:15,311 --> 00:57:16,757
- [Tom] No, I take that back.

1764
00:57:16,757 --> 00:57:17,590
- Come in.

1765
00:57:18,677 --> 00:57:20,379
- [Mike] Boy, what a day I've had.

1766
00:57:20,379 --> 00:57:21,915
I gotta say, I'm all creeped out.

1767
00:57:21,915 --> 00:57:24,283
- I was worried about you.

1768
00:57:24,283 --> 00:57:27,010
I mean, those men who were after you.

1769
00:57:27,010 --> 00:57:28,597
- Thanks for helping me.

1770
00:57:28,597 --> 00:57:29,967
I brought you something.

1771
00:57:29,967 --> 00:57:31,439
- Oh, thank you.

1772
00:57:31,439 --> 00:57:32,356
What is it?

1773
00:57:33,371 --> 00:57:35,816
- Why don't you look at it?

1774
00:57:35,816 --> 00:57:38,056
- You see, I'm blind.

1775
00:57:38,056 --> 00:57:39,746
- [Tom] Lemon Jefferson.

1776
00:57:39,746 --> 00:57:40,733
- Blind?

1777
00:57:40,733 --> 00:57:41,704
- [Tom] Lead me on.

1778
00:57:41,704 --> 00:57:44,597
- I thought you might have
guessed it last night.

1779
00:57:44,597 --> 00:57:46,430
What did you bring me?

1780
00:57:47,413 --> 00:57:48,360
- Here.

1781
00:57:48,360 --> 00:57:50,587
- [Crow] It's a View-Master.

1782
00:57:50,587 --> 00:57:53,493
- [Tom] Did you keep the receipt?

1783
00:57:53,493 --> 00:57:56,435
- Oh, it's beautiful, I can tell.

1784
00:57:56,435 --> 00:57:59,176
(strained orchestral music)

1785
00:57:59,176 --> 00:58:00,648
Please, let's sit down.

1786
00:58:00,648 --> 00:58:03,106
- [Crow] Oh, does he string
krill through his mouth?

1787
00:58:03,106 --> 00:58:04,719
- [Mike] Big baleen.

1788
00:58:04,719 --> 00:58:08,251
- [Tom] So do we make out now, or?

1789
00:58:08,251 --> 00:58:10,953
- Why were those men chasing you?

1790
00:58:10,953 --> 00:58:12,770
- They'd thought I'd done something,

1791
00:58:12,770 --> 00:58:14,639
but it wasn't my fault.

1792
00:58:14,639 --> 00:58:16,085
- Oh, I'm sure it wasn't.

1793
00:58:16,085 --> 00:58:18,235
- [Mike] Creeper, may I call you "creep"?

1794
00:58:18,235 --> 00:58:21,013
- I don't know if I should
accept this from you.

1795
00:58:21,013 --> 00:58:23,112
You haven't even told me your name.

1796
00:58:23,112 --> 00:58:24,533
- It's Hal.

1797
00:58:24,533 --> 00:58:25,915
- Mine's Helen.

1798
00:58:25,915 --> 00:58:27,285
- [Crow] We're engaged now, right?

1799
00:58:27,285 --> 00:58:29,013
- Last night you seemed surprised because

1800
00:58:29,013 --> 00:58:31,163
I wasn't afraid of you.

1801
00:58:31,163 --> 00:58:32,968
What's wrong, Hal?

1802
00:58:32,968 --> 00:58:34,248
Should I be afraid?

1803
00:58:34,248 --> 00:58:35,362
- [Tom] Might be a good idea.

1804
00:58:35,362 --> 00:58:36,655
- Everyone else is.

1805
00:58:36,655 --> 00:58:38,152
- [Mike] Well, now I am.
- [Helen] Why?

1806
00:58:38,152 --> 00:58:39,791
- I can't tell you.

1807
00:58:39,791 --> 00:58:41,186
- [Crow] It's a surprise.

1808
00:58:41,186 --> 00:58:42,490
- Do you wanna be friends with me?

1809
00:58:42,490 --> 00:58:45,070
- [Tom] Gee, I don't know,
I have so many, well, okay.

1810
00:58:45,070 --> 00:58:47,945
- Sure, you're nice to me.

1811
00:58:47,945 --> 00:58:51,120
- And no one else is, is that it?

1812
00:58:51,120 --> 00:58:54,320
- [Tom] The coy Rondo Hatton.

1813
00:58:54,320 --> 00:58:55,779
- I think I understand.

1814
00:58:55,779 --> 00:58:58,224
- [Crow] You're a hideous murderer.

1815
00:58:58,224 --> 00:59:00,643
- When you've been blind as long as I have

1816
00:59:00,643 --> 00:59:02,576
you learn to see through your senses.

1817
00:59:02,576 --> 00:59:04,214
- [Mike] I sense through my sweat glands.

1818
00:59:04,214 --> 00:59:07,004
- I can't explain it exactly,
but you get a feeling

1819
00:59:07,004 --> 00:59:10,084
about people when you meet them.

1820
00:59:10,084 --> 00:59:12,671
You see a picture of them in your mind.

1821
00:59:12,671 --> 00:59:13,738
- [Tom] Picturing a grouper.

1822
00:59:13,738 --> 00:59:16,124
- Not just what they look
like, but what they really are.

1823
00:59:16,124 --> 00:59:17,831
- [Crow] And what size hat they wear.

1824
00:59:17,831 --> 00:59:19,138
- You see, them much more clearly

1825
00:59:19,138 --> 00:59:20,618
then you do with your eyes.

1826
00:59:20,618 --> 00:59:21,831
- [Tom] No, you don't.

1827
00:59:21,831 --> 00:59:24,311
- Maybe that's why they
say "looks are deceptive".

1828
00:59:24,311 --> 00:59:25,604
- [Tom] No, they're not.

1829
00:59:25,604 --> 00:59:26,859
- Do you know what I mean?

1830
00:59:26,859 --> 00:59:28,284
- [Crow] You're smothering me.

1831
00:59:28,284 --> 00:59:29,451
- Yes, I know.

1832
00:59:30,604 --> 00:59:32,871
- I'd like to help you, if you'd let me.

1833
00:59:32,871 --> 00:59:33,831
- You can.

1834
00:59:33,831 --> 00:59:34,951
- How?

1835
00:59:34,951 --> 00:59:36,724
- Just let me come to see you.

1836
00:59:36,724 --> 00:59:39,111
- Of course, I want you to.

1837
00:59:39,111 --> 00:59:40,617
I'm alone most of the time, except

1838
00:59:40,617 --> 00:59:42,324
when I give piano lessons.

1839
00:59:42,324 --> 00:59:43,951
- [Tom] Or when I'm at umpire school.

1840
00:59:43,951 --> 00:59:46,831
- I have such a good idea of you now,

1841
00:59:46,831 --> 00:59:48,244
but if I could touch your face, then--

1842
00:59:48,244 --> 00:59:50,711
- No, don't do that.

1843
00:59:50,711 --> 00:59:51,991
- Why not?

1844
00:59:51,991 --> 00:59:53,324
- I don't want you to.

1845
00:59:53,324 --> 00:59:54,871
- [Crow] I'm breaking out, okay?

1846
00:59:54,871 --> 00:59:55,704
- Hal.

1847
00:59:55,704 --> 00:59:58,591
- [Mike] You're wearing
Drakkar Noir, aren't you?

1848
00:59:58,591 --> 01:00:00,049
(door shuts)
- Hal.

1849
01:00:00,049 --> 01:00:02,738
- [Tom] Open the pod bay door, Hal.

1850
01:00:02,738 --> 01:00:04,738
Hmm, Miss Helen Creeper.

1851
01:00:06,044 --> 01:00:08,738
(strained orchestra music)

1852
01:00:08,738 --> 01:00:10,391
(moves to ominous orchestral music)

1853
01:00:10,391 --> 01:00:13,298
- [Crow] Refused to use articles.

1854
01:00:13,298 --> 01:00:16,014
- [Tom] A police wrong.

1855
01:00:16,014 --> 01:00:19,251
(scary orchestra music)

1856
01:00:19,251 --> 01:00:21,892
- [Crow] Or your money back!

1857
01:00:21,892 --> 01:00:23,921
(ominous orchestral music)

1858
01:00:23,921 --> 01:00:27,571
- [Mike] The haunting creeper theme.

1859
01:00:27,571 --> 01:00:29,078
- [Crow] Come on, I
didn't mean to break it.

1860
01:00:29,078 --> 01:00:31,161
I thought it was in park.

1861
01:00:34,798 --> 01:00:36,224
- Well, good morning, Commissioner.

1862
01:00:36,224 --> 01:00:37,998
Good morning, Mr. Parkington.

1863
01:00:37,998 --> 01:00:40,251
I thought you'd be dropping
by about this time.

1864
01:00:40,251 --> 01:00:41,877
- So, you're sitting
here playing Gin Rummy

1865
01:00:41,877 --> 01:00:43,597
with another creeper
murder on the front page

1866
01:00:43,597 --> 01:00:44,637
of the newspapers.

1867
01:00:44,637 --> 01:00:47,157
- I think better when I'm enjoy my hobby.

1868
01:00:47,157 --> 01:00:49,682
I'm a pigeon fancier.

1869
01:00:49,682 --> 01:00:50,515
- [Tom] What is this scene for?

1870
01:00:50,515 --> 01:00:51,755
- Now, listen to me,
Donelly, we told you--

1871
01:00:51,755 --> 01:00:53,275
- I know, I know, you told me already

1872
01:00:53,275 --> 01:00:56,021
that you wanted the creeper
arrested within 24 hours.

1873
01:00:56,021 --> 01:00:58,875
Well, gentlemen, he's still at large.

1874
01:00:58,875 --> 01:01:00,848
- Now, I suppose the bear
and yourself have decided

1875
01:01:00,848 --> 01:01:02,701
to take over, personally.

1876
01:01:02,701 --> 01:01:04,528
No doubt, you'll have
the killer in custody in

1877
01:01:04,528 --> 01:01:06,355
10 minutes flat.

1878
01:01:06,355 --> 01:01:08,395
I'll just let the newspapers
know that it's in your lap.

1879
01:01:08,395 --> 01:01:09,755
- Now, just a minute, Captain, let's not

1880
01:01:09,755 --> 01:01:11,088
be hasty about this.

1881
01:01:11,088 --> 01:01:12,635
- You don't mean, that
his honor, the mayor,

1882
01:01:12,635 --> 01:01:14,195
wouldn't relish the job,
that you wouldn't like

1883
01:01:14,195 --> 01:01:15,275
to take over?

1884
01:01:15,275 --> 01:01:16,901
- That's beside the point, the entire city

1885
01:01:16,901 --> 01:01:19,449
is up in arms, and the creeper
goes on killing people.

1886
01:01:19,449 --> 01:01:21,195
Now, I'm a reasonable man, Donelly.

1887
01:01:21,195 --> 01:01:22,821
- I'm glad to hear it, Commissioner.

1888
01:01:22,821 --> 01:01:23,955
- [Mike] It's uncle Charlie.

1889
01:01:23,955 --> 01:01:25,315
- Now that the pressure's
off, I can let you know

1890
01:01:25,315 --> 01:01:27,168
that we have the first definite clues

1891
01:01:27,168 --> 01:01:28,528
to the identity of the creeper.

1892
01:01:28,528 --> 01:01:29,361
- Who is it?

1893
01:01:29,361 --> 01:01:30,248
- Yes, who is it?

1894
01:01:30,248 --> 01:01:31,901
- I said "clue", Gentlemen.

1895
01:01:31,901 --> 01:01:33,728
When I'm certain, I'll
give you the lowdown,

1896
01:01:33,728 --> 01:01:35,701
and I believe it'll be soon.

1897
01:01:35,701 --> 01:01:37,848
That's all I can tell you at the moment.

1898
01:01:37,848 --> 01:01:40,581
Now, if you'll excuse
me, I have Gates on the

1899
01:01:40,581 --> 01:01:41,741
triple blitz.

1900
01:01:41,741 --> 01:01:42,928
- [Crow] I'm beginning to lose my trust

1901
01:01:42,928 --> 01:01:44,008
in government.

1902
01:01:44,008 --> 01:01:45,395
- [Tom] Nice to know that Noel Coward's

1903
01:01:45,395 --> 01:01:47,270
in charge of the police force.

1904
01:01:47,270 --> 01:01:48,155
(door closes)

1905
01:01:48,155 --> 01:01:48,988
- Why didn't you let them know

1906
01:01:48,988 --> 01:01:50,085
about the newspaper clippings?

1907
01:01:50,085 --> 01:01:51,938
- What, and have those publicity hounds

1908
01:01:51,938 --> 01:01:53,618
filling the front pages
with a lot of stuff

1909
01:01:53,618 --> 01:01:57,653
that would scare the
creeper right out of town?

1910
01:01:57,653 --> 01:02:00,486
(synth pop music)

1911
01:02:03,553 --> 01:02:05,931
(moves to ominous orchestral music)

1912
01:02:05,931 --> 01:02:08,208
- [Mike] Well, that sex
was very serviceable

1913
01:02:08,208 --> 01:02:10,303
and reliable, thank you.

1914
01:02:10,303 --> 01:02:11,581
- [Tom] Why, hello, creeper.

1915
01:02:11,581 --> 01:02:12,768
- [Crow] Hi, creeper.

1916
01:02:12,768 --> 01:02:14,218
- [Tom] Hi.

1917
01:02:14,218 --> 01:02:15,955
(ominous orchestral music)

1918
01:02:15,955 --> 01:02:17,091
What, did this take place in Alaska?

1919
01:02:17,091 --> 01:02:18,545
There's no daylight.

1920
01:02:18,545 --> 01:02:19,998
- [Crow] I can't believe
she makes me go outside

1921
01:02:19,998 --> 01:02:21,748
to smoke a cigarette.

1922
01:02:23,371 --> 01:02:25,305
- [Mike] I'd better go
drop off the instructions

1923
01:02:25,305 --> 01:02:27,148
for the brooch.

1924
01:02:27,148 --> 01:02:30,406
- [Tom] Welcome to the
creeper arms apartments.

1925
01:02:30,406 --> 01:02:33,855
(ominous orchestral music)

1926
01:02:33,855 --> 01:02:37,386
- One and two and three and four and--

1927
01:02:37,386 --> 01:02:39,633
- [Crow] She's not a
very good singer, is she?

1928
01:02:39,633 --> 01:02:41,160
- [Mike] The song's got something to do

1929
01:02:41,160 --> 01:02:42,019
with it, too.

1930
01:02:42,019 --> 01:02:45,006
- One and two and three and four and

1931
01:02:45,006 --> 01:02:48,686
one and two and three and four and,

1932
01:02:48,686 --> 01:02:52,086
that's find, Dorothy, just fine.

1933
01:02:52,086 --> 01:02:53,339
- [Mike] That'll be 600 dollars.

1934
01:02:53,339 --> 01:02:55,343
- She's coming along very
nicely, Mrs. Obringer.

1935
01:02:55,343 --> 01:02:56,176
- [Tom] She is?

1936
01:02:56,176 --> 01:02:57,663
- You'll be playing the
classics before you know it.

1937
01:02:57,663 --> 01:02:59,143
- I don't wanna play that stuff,

1938
01:02:59,143 --> 01:03:00,569
I wanna play boogie-woogie.

1939
01:03:00,569 --> 01:03:03,144
- Well, you can play
that, too, as a hobby,

1940
01:03:03,144 --> 01:03:04,663
but first, you must master the things

1941
01:03:04,663 --> 01:03:05,496
that are solid.

1942
01:03:05,496 --> 01:03:08,863
- But boogie-woogie is
solid, really sensual.

1943
01:03:08,863 --> 01:03:10,756
- Hush, dear.

1944
01:03:10,756 --> 01:03:13,569
Put your music away, we're going home now.

1945
01:03:13,569 --> 01:03:15,196
She get's that from her father, he plays

1946
01:03:15,196 --> 01:03:17,663
hot trumpet in the fireman's band.

1947
01:03:17,663 --> 01:03:19,080
Come along, dear.

1948
01:03:20,649 --> 01:03:21,929
- [Tom] Why do you not open, door?

1949
01:03:21,929 --> 01:03:22,762
(door opens)

1950
01:03:22,762 --> 01:03:24,769
- We'll see you next
week at the same time,

1951
01:03:24,769 --> 01:03:26,516
I'll see that she practices like you

1952
01:03:26,516 --> 01:03:27,876
told her to, Miss Helen.

1953
01:03:27,876 --> 01:03:29,183
- I'll be right in the groove.

1954
01:03:29,183 --> 01:03:31,063
- Oh, Dorothy.

1955
01:03:31,063 --> 01:03:31,896
Good night.

1956
01:03:31,896 --> 01:03:32,979
- Good night.

1957
01:03:34,079 --> 01:03:35,679
- [Crow] Hey, fellow, why the long face?

1958
01:03:35,679 --> 01:03:36,512
- [Mike] Oh, now, come on.

1959
01:03:36,512 --> 01:03:37,747
- [Crow] (laughing) I'm sorry.

1960
01:03:37,747 --> 01:03:38,933
- [Mike] I begged you not to do that.

1961
01:03:38,933 --> 01:03:39,853
- [Crow] No.

1962
01:03:39,853 --> 01:03:41,653
- [Tom] That hurts.

1963
01:03:41,653 --> 01:03:43,703
If the hands of time.

1964
01:03:43,703 --> 01:03:45,109
(sad piano music)

1965
01:03:45,109 --> 01:03:48,844
- [Mike] You have a
real problem with that.

1966
01:03:48,844 --> 01:03:50,511
- [Crow] Hi, Amaire.

1967
01:03:51,996 --> 01:03:53,676
- [Tom] Uh, you want to
use a different fingering

1968
01:03:53,676 --> 01:03:55,996
not to break up the phrasing.

1969
01:03:55,996 --> 01:03:58,356
Uh, look, Helen, other
girls in the sorority

1970
01:03:58,356 --> 01:04:00,329
asked me to talk to you
about your incessant

1971
01:04:00,329 --> 01:04:01,689
piano playing.

1972
01:04:01,689 --> 01:04:02,876
- [Crow] She can't hear him?

1973
01:04:02,876 --> 01:04:04,503
She's the most inefficient blind person

1974
01:04:04,503 --> 01:04:05,753
I've ever seen.

1975
01:04:06,743 --> 01:04:08,249
- [Tom] Boy, you think you're God's gift

1976
01:04:08,249 --> 01:04:09,999
to pianos, don't you?

1977
01:04:11,149 --> 01:04:12,956
(soft orchestral music)

1978
01:04:12,956 --> 01:04:13,799
- Who is it?

1979
01:04:13,799 --> 01:04:15,226
- Hello.

1980
01:04:15,226 --> 01:04:16,639
- Oh, it's you, Hal.

1981
01:04:16,639 --> 01:04:18,266
I didn't hear you come in.

1982
01:04:18,266 --> 01:04:19,279
- I didn't knock.

1983
01:04:19,279 --> 01:04:20,413
- [Tom] I creeped.

1984
01:04:20,413 --> 01:04:22,066
- Are those men after you again?

1985
01:04:22,066 --> 01:04:24,719
- No, I was listening outside.

1986
01:04:24,719 --> 01:04:27,679
When those people left, I just walked in.

1987
01:04:27,679 --> 01:04:29,359
- Please, sit down.

1988
01:04:29,359 --> 01:04:33,333
- [Mike] I'm going to call the police.

1989
01:04:33,333 --> 01:04:35,747
- You make a living teaching kids to play?

1990
01:04:35,747 --> 01:04:37,693
- Oh, yes, I manage to get along.

1991
01:04:37,693 --> 01:04:39,253
- [Tom] How do you get
into that kind of thing?

1992
01:04:39,253 --> 01:04:40,973
- It's tough, doing that, you being

1993
01:04:40,973 --> 01:04:42,799
like you are.

1994
01:04:42,799 --> 01:04:46,079
- I love music, and I like
teaching it to others.

1995
01:04:46,079 --> 01:04:47,466
I don't fine my blindness too much

1996
01:04:47,466 --> 01:04:49,119
of a handicap.

1997
01:04:49,119 --> 01:04:50,493
- [Mike] Yeah, me, I'm a killer.

1998
01:04:50,493 --> 01:04:52,573
- Would you ever be able to see?

1999
01:04:52,573 --> 01:04:54,173
- [Tom] No, now, lay off!

2000
01:04:54,173 --> 01:04:56,066
- The doctor told me a year ago that

2001
01:04:56,066 --> 01:04:59,053
there might be a chance,
if I had an operation.

2002
01:04:59,053 --> 01:05:00,853
- Why don't you do it?

2003
01:05:00,853 --> 01:05:02,653
- Oh, it would be very expensive.

2004
